INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anaemia — Approach & IDA Management','easy','mcq','A 34-year-old woman presents with progressive fatigue, pallor, and dyspnoea on exertion. Her FBC shows Hb 9.2 g/dL, MCV 68 fL, MCH 22 pg. Serum ferritin is 6 ng/mL. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A) Iron deficiency anaemia", "B) Anaemia of chronic disease", "C) Beta-thalassaemia trait", "D) Sideroblastic anaemia"]','A','Iron deficiency anaemia is characterised by a microcytic hypochromic picture (low MCV and MCH) combined with a low serum ferritin, which reflects depleted iron stores. Anaemia of chronic disease typically shows normal or elevated ferritin. The clinical picture here is consistent with IDA.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anaemia — Approach & IDA Management','medium','mcq','A 28-year-old woman is found to have Hb 10.1 g/dL, MCV 71 fL, serum iron 7 µmol/L, TIBC 72 µmol/L, and serum ferritin 8 ng/mL. Which iron study pattern best confirms iron deficiency anaemia?','["A) High ferritin, low TIBC, low serum iron", "B) Low ferritin, high TIBC, low serum iron", "C) Normal ferritin, low TIBC, low serum iron", "D) Low ferritin, low TIBC, high serum iron"]','B','In iron deficiency anaemia, ferritin is low (reflecting depleted stores), serum iron is low (insufficient iron for erythropoiesis), and TIBC is elevated as transferrin is upregulated to capture any available iron. This triad distinguishes IDA from anaemia of chronic disease, where ferritin is normal or raised and TIBC is low.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anaemia — Approach & IDA Management','medium','mcq','A 45-year-old man with Hb 8.6 g/dL and confirmed iron deficiency anaemia is started on ferrous sulphate. According to the Ghana Standard Treatment Guidelines, what is the correct adult dose?','["A) Ferrous sulphate 200 mg once daily for 1 month", "B) Ferrous gluconate 300 mg twice daily for 6 weeks", "C) Ferrous sulphate 200 mg 8 hourly for 3–6 months", "D) Ferrous sulphate 200 mg twice daily for 2 weeks"]','C','The Ghana STG recommends ferrous sulphate 200 mg (providing 65 mg elemental iron) taken three times daily (8 hourly) for 3–6 months to replenish iron stores in adults. Treatment should continue for at least 3 months after haemoglobin normalises to ensure adequate storage iron repletion.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anaemia — Approach & IDA Management','hard','mcq','A 52-year-old woman with coeliac disease and iron deficiency anaemia fails to respond to three months of oral ferrous sulphate. Her Hb remains 8.0 g/dL and ferritin 5 ng/mL. What is the most appropriate next step?','["A) Switch to ferrous gluconate and continue for another 3 months", "B) Add vitamin C supplementation and recheck in 1 month", "C) Commence a blood transfusion immediately", "D) Initiate parenteral iron infusion"]','D','Parenteral iron is indicated when oral iron therapy fails due to malabsorption, as seen in coeliac disease, inflammatory bowel disease, or post-gastrectomy states. In this patient with confirmed malabsorption, IV iron (such as ferric carboxymaltose or iron sucrose) bypasses the gastrointestinal tract and restores iron stores effectively. Blood transfusion is reserved for severe symptomatic anaemia.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anaemia — Approach & IDA Management','easy','mcq','According to WHO criteria, which Hb threshold defines anaemia in an adult non-pregnant female?','["A) Hb < 12 g/dL", "B) Hb < 13 g/dL", "C) Hb < 11 g/dL", "D) Hb < 10 g/dL"]','A','The WHO defines anaemia in adult non-pregnant females as Hb below 12 g/dL, in adult males as below 13 g/dL, and in children 6 months to 5 years as below 11 g/dL. These thresholds are used globally including in Ghana STG guidelines to guide diagnosis and treatment decisions.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anaemia — Approach & IDA Management','medium','mcq','A 30-year-old woman started on ferrous sulphate for IDA asks when she can expect to see a haematological response. Which statement is most accurate?','["A) Hb rises immediately within 24–48 hours of starting iron", "B) Reticulocyte count rises at 5–10 days; Hb rises approximately 1 g/dL per week", "C) Hb typically normalises within 2 weeks of starting oral iron", "D) Reticulocyte response occurs after 4–6 weeks of iron therapy"]','B','After initiating oral iron therapy, the reticulocyte count begins to rise within 5–10 days, reflecting increased erythropoiesis. Haemoglobin typically increases by approximately 1 g/dL per week thereafter. Full normalisation of Hb takes 4–8 weeks, but therapy must continue for 3–6 months to replenish iron stores.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anaemia — Approach & IDA Management','medium','mcq','A 68-year-old man presents with Hb 6.8 g/dL, heart rate 112 bpm, and chest pain on minimal exertion. Iron studies confirm IDA. What is the most appropriate immediate management?','["A) Start oral ferrous sulphate and review in 4 weeks", "B) Give IM iron and review haematological response in 2 weeks", "C) Transfuse packed red blood cells urgently", "D) Start parenteral iron infusion and admit for monitoring"]','C','Severe symptomatic anaemia with cardiovascular compromise (Hb <7 g/dL with chest pain and tachycardia) requires immediate packed red cell transfusion to restore oxygen delivery. Oral or parenteral iron is appropriate for non-urgent IDA but will not reverse life-threatening haemodynamic compromise quickly enough. After stabilisation, the underlying cause and iron replacement should be addressed.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anaemia — Approach & IDA Management','hard','mcq','A 55-year-old man is found to have microcytic anaemia with Hb 9.4 g/dL, MCV 72 fL, serum ferritin 95 ng/mL, serum iron 8 µmol/L, and TIBC 28 µmol/L. He has rheumatoid arthritis on methotrexate. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A) Iron deficiency anaemia", "B) Beta-thalassaemia trait", "C) Sideroblastic anaemia", "D) Anaemia of chronic disease"]','D','Anaemia of chronic disease (ACD) is characterised by low serum iron and low TIBC (unlike IDA where TIBC is high), with a normal or elevated ferritin reflecting sequestration of iron within macrophages by hepcidin. In this patient with rheumatoid arthritis, ACD is the most consistent diagnosis. IDA would show low ferritin and high TIBC.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anaemia — Approach & IDA Management','easy','mcq','A peripheral blood film from a patient with IDA is most likely to show which of the following?','["A) Microcytes, hypochromic red cells, and pencil cells", "B) Macrocytes and hypersegmented neutrophils", "C) Spherocytes and polychromasia", "D) Schistocytes and helmet cells"]','A','Iron deficiency anaemia produces a microcytic, hypochromic blood film with pencil cells (elliptocytes), target cells, and anisocytosis. Macrocytes and hypersegmented neutrophils indicate megaloblastic anaemia (B12 or folate deficiency). Spherocytes suggest haemolysis, and schistocytes point to microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anaemia — Approach & IDA Management','medium','mcq','A 22-year-old pregnant woman at 28 weeks gestation has Hb 10.4 g/dL and confirmed IDA. She is started on ferrous sulphate. Which additional supplement is routinely co-prescribed in pregnancy?','["A) Vitamin B12 1 mg IM", "B) Folic acid 5 mg daily", "C) Vitamin C 500 mg daily", "D) Pyridoxine 50 mg daily"]','B','Folic acid 5 mg daily is co-prescribed with iron in pregnant women with IDA because folate requirements increase significantly in pregnancy and folate deficiency can co-exist with or mask IDA. Folic acid supplementation also reduces the risk of neural tube defects. This combination is recommended in both Ghana STG and WHO guidelines.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','B12/Folate Deficiency Management','medium','mcq','A 62-year-old vegan woman presents with fatigue, numbness in both feet, and unsteady gait. Hb is 8.2 g/dL, MCV 112 fL. Blood film shows hypersegmented neutrophils. Serum B12 is 145 pg/mL. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A) Folate deficiency anaemia", "B) Hypothyroidism with macrocytic anaemia", "C) B12 deficiency with subacute combined degeneration of the cord", "D) Alcohol-related macrocytosis"]','C','This patient has B12 deficiency presenting with megaloblastic anaemia (macrocytosis, hypersegmented neutrophils) and neurological features consistent with subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord (posterior and lateral column involvement causing peripheral neuropathy and ataxia). Folate deficiency produces an identical blood picture but does NOT cause neurological features, making B12 deficiency the diagnosis here.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','B12/Folate Deficiency Management','hard','mcq','A 70-year-old man with pernicious anaemia is diagnosed with B12 deficiency. Which finding would most specifically support pernicious anaemia as the aetiology?','["A) Serum B12 < 200 pg/mL", "B) MCV > 100 fL on FBC", "C) Elevated homocysteine level", "D) Positive anti-intrinsic factor antibodies"]','D','Anti-intrinsic factor (anti-IF) antibodies are highly specific (>95%) for pernicious anaemia, although sensitivity is only ~50%. They confirm immune-mediated destruction of gastric parietal cells leading to intrinsic factor deficiency and B12 malabsorption. Macrocytosis, low B12, and elevated homocysteine are common to all causes of B12 deficiency and are not specific to pernicious anaemia.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','B12/Folate Deficiency Management','medium','mcq','A 58-year-old man is diagnosed with pernicious anaemia and B12 deficiency. According to standard treatment guidelines, what is the correct initial regimen for hydroxocobalamin?','["A) Hydroxocobalamin 1 mg IM every other day for 6 doses, then 1 mg every 3 months for life", "B) Hydroxocobalamin 1 mg IM daily for 10 days, then monthly", "C) Cyanocobalamin 500 mcg oral daily for 3 months, then stop", "D) Hydroxocobalamin 1 mg IM weekly for 4 weeks, then annually"]','A','The Ghana STG (and British National Formulary) recommends hydroxocobalamin 1 mg IM every other day for 6 doses (loading phase) followed by maintenance dosing of 1 mg IM every 3 months for life in patients with pernicious anaemia. Oral B12 is not appropriate when intrinsic factor is absent as absorption requires IF. Lifelong maintenance is required since the underlying cause cannot be reversed.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','B12/Folate Deficiency Management','easy','mcq','A 35-year-old woman with megaloblastic anaemia and no neurological symptoms is found to have normal serum B12 but low serum folate. What is the first-line treatment?','["A) Hydroxocobalamin 1 mg IM every other day", "B) Folic acid 5 mg orally daily", "C) Folic acid 1 mg orally daily for 2 weeks", "D) Parenteral folate 15 mg IV for 5 days"]','B','Folate deficiency is treated with folic acid 5 mg orally daily for 4 months (or longer if the underlying cause persists). Before starting folic acid, B12 deficiency must be excluded or treated simultaneously, as folic acid can correct the haematological manifestations of B12 deficiency while allowing neurological deterioration to continue. There is no parenteral folate formulation used routinely.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','B12/Folate Deficiency Management','medium','mcq','A 45-year-old man with newly diagnosed B12 deficiency anaemia is started on hydroxocobalamin IM injections. He asks why he cannot take oral B12 tablets. Which explanation is most accurate?','["A) Oral B12 is rapidly degraded by gastric acid before absorption", "B) Oral B12 causes severe gastrointestinal side effects at therapeutic doses", "C) B12 absorption in the terminal ileum requires intrinsic factor, which is absent in his condition", "D) Oral B12 does not cross the blood-brain barrier and cannot reverse neurological damage"]','C','Vitamin B12 is absorbed in the terminal ileum via a receptor that requires intrinsic factor (IF) produced by gastric parietal cells. In pernicious anaemia and post-gastrectomy states, IF is absent, making oral B12 ineffective. Parenteral (IM) administration bypasses this requirement. High-dose oral B12 (1000 mcg daily) can achieve some passive absorption but is not standard in confirmed IF deficiency.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','B12/Folate Deficiency Management','hard','mcq','A 48-year-old woman with folate deficiency anaemia is started on folic acid 5 mg daily. Two weeks later her reticulocyte count peaks. However, her serum B12 is subsequently found to be 110 pg/mL. She now complains of tingling in her fingers. What error occurred?','["A) Folic acid was given at too high a dose causing B12 depletion", "B) Folate therapy induced B12 deficiency by competitive inhibition of absorption", "C) The reticulocyte peak confirms adequate treatment and neuropathy is unrelated", "D) Folate supplementation corrected haematological features while masking and worsening B12 deficiency neuropathy"]','D','This is a classic clinical pitfall: giving folic acid to a patient with undetected B12 deficiency corrects the megaloblastic anaemia (masking the haematological clue) but does not treat, and may accelerate, B12 deficiency neuropathy. This is why B12 must be measured and excluded or treated before or alongside folate supplementation. The mechanism is not competitive inhibition but rather masked diagnosis.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','B12/Folate Deficiency Management','easy','mcq','What MCV value on FBC would raise suspicion for megaloblastic anaemia?','["A) MCV > 100 fL", "B) MCV 85–95 fL", "C) MCV 70–80 fL", "D) MCV < 70 fL"]','A','Megaloblastic anaemia (due to B12 or folate deficiency) characteristically produces macrocytosis with MCV >100 fL (normal 80–100 fL). Values >115 fL strongly suggest megaloblastic anaemia or liver disease. Microcytosis (MCV <80 fL) suggests iron deficiency, thalassaemia, or sideroblastic anaemia.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','B12/Folate Deficiency Management','medium','mcq','A 67-year-old man had a total gastrectomy 2 years ago and now presents with Hb 9.8 g/dL, MCV 108 fL, and serum B12 of 160 pg/mL. What is the mechanism of his B12 deficiency?','["A) Dietary insufficiency due to post-surgical dietary restrictions", "B) Loss of intrinsic factor production leading to B12 malabsorption", "C) Accelerated B12 utilisation due to post-gastrectomy bacterial overgrowth", "D) Autoimmune destruction of ileal B12 receptors post-surgery"]','B','Total gastrectomy removes all gastric parietal cells, which are the sole source of intrinsic factor. Without intrinsic factor, dietary B12 cannot be absorbed in the terminal ileum. All patients post-total gastrectomy require lifelong parenteral B12 supplementation. Bacterial overgrowth can also cause B12 deficiency but is secondary here; the primary mechanism is loss of intrinsic factor.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','B12/Folate Deficiency Management','hard','mcq','A 55-year-old woman with terminal ileal Crohn''s disease and megaloblastic anaemia has serum B12 of 130 pg/mL. She denies dietary restrictions and her anti-intrinsic factor antibodies are negative. What is the most likely cause of her B12 deficiency?','["A) Pernicious anaemia with false-negative anti-IF antibodies", "B) Dietary deficiency secondary to disease-related anorexia", "C) Malabsorption of B12 due to terminal ileal disease", "D) Folate-induced B12 depletion from her current medication"]','C','The terminal ileum is the exclusive site of vitamin B12 absorption (via the cubilin receptor requiring intrinsic factor). Crohn''s disease affecting the terminal ileum disrupts B12 absorption regardless of intrinsic factor availability. This is distinct from pernicious anaemia. Management requires parenteral hydroxocobalamin rather than oral supplementation.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','B12/Folate Deficiency Management','medium','mcq','A 72-year-old woman with pernicious anaemia on maintenance hydroxocobalamin injections every 3 months attends for review. Her Hb is 13.2 g/dL and MCV is 94 fL. She asks if she can stop injections now that her blood count is normal. What is the best response?','["A) She can stop injections as her Hb and MCV have normalised", "B) Injections can be spaced to every 6 months given her normal results", "C) She can switch to oral B12 1000 mcg daily as maintenance", "D) She should continue injections every 3 months for life as the underlying cause is irreversible"]','D','Pernicious anaemia is caused by autoimmune destruction of gastric parietal cells, which is irreversible. Intrinsic factor will never be produced again, so oral B12 absorption remains permanently impaired. Patients require lifelong parenteral hydroxocobalamin every 3 months. Stopping or reducing treatment risks recurrence of both haematological and neurological complications.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Haemolytic Anaemia — Clinical Management','medium','mcq','A 38-year-old woman presents with jaundice, dark urine, and fatigue. Hb is 7.4 g/dL, reticulocyte count 12%, bilirubin 64 µmol/L (unconjugated), LDH 820 U/L, and haptoglobin is undetectable. Direct Coombs test is positive. What is the diagnosis?','["A) Autoimmune haemolytic anaemia", "B) Hereditary spherocytosis", "C) G6PD deficiency haemolytic crisis", "D) Microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia"]','A','Autoimmune haemolytic anaemia (AIHA) is confirmed by a positive direct Coombs (direct antiglobulin) test, which detects IgG or complement on the red cell surface. The clinical picture of haemolysis — elevated unconjugated bilirubin, raised LDH, elevated reticulocytes, and low haptoglobin — supports this diagnosis. Hereditary spherocytosis is Coombs negative; MAHA shows schistocytes.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Haemolytic Anaemia — Clinical Management','medium','mcq','A 42-year-old woman is diagnosed with warm autoimmune haemolytic anaemia (IgG-mediated). What is the first-line treatment?','["A) Splenectomy", "B) Prednisolone 1 mg/kg/day orally", "C) Rituximab 375 mg/m² IV weekly for 4 doses", "D) Azathioprine 2 mg/kg/day"]','B','Warm AIHA (IgG-mediated, occurring at body temperature) is treated first-line with prednisolone 1 mg/kg/day. Most patients respond within 1–3 weeks. Steroids are tapered slowly once remission is achieved. Splenectomy is reserved for steroid-refractory or steroid-dependent cases. Rituximab is a second-line option. Azathioprine is used as a steroid-sparing agent.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Haemolytic Anaemia — Clinical Management','hard','mcq','A 65-year-old man develops Hb 6.2 g/dL with haemolysis after starting dapsone for dermatitis herpetiformis. Blood film shows Heinz bodies and bite cells. G6PD assay is markedly reduced. What is the immediate management?','["A) Perform exchange transfusion urgently", "B) Start rituximab and refer to haematology", "C) Stop dapsone, give supportive care, and transfuse if Hb < 7 g/dL", "D) Start prednisolone 1 mg/kg and continue dapsone"]','C','G6PD deficiency haemolytic crisis is triggered by oxidant drugs including dapsone, primaquine, nitrofurantoin, and high-dose aspirin. Management centres on immediate withdrawal of the offending drug. Supportive care including hydration, folic acid supplementation, and red cell transfusion (if Hb <7 g/dL or haemodynamically compromised) is the mainstay. Steroids and rituximab have no role in G6PD haemolysis.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Haemolytic Anaemia — Clinical Management','easy','mcq','Which combination of laboratory findings is most consistent with haemolytic anaemia?','["A) Low LDH, elevated haptoglobin, direct Coombs positive", "B) Elevated conjugated bilirubin, low reticulocyte count, normal LDH", "C) Low MCV, elevated ferritin, low reticulocyte count", "D) Elevated unconjugated bilirubin, raised LDH, low haptoglobin, elevated reticulocytes"]','D','Haemolysis is characterised by raised unconjugated bilirubin (from haem catabolism), elevated LDH (released from lysed RBCs), low haptoglobin (consumed binding free haemoglobin), elevated reticulocyte count (compensatory erythropoiesis), and haemoglobinuria in intravascular haemolysis. Elevated conjugated bilirubin suggests hepatic or post-hepatic disease rather than haemolysis.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Haemolytic Anaemia — Clinical Management','medium','mcq','A 28-year-old man with hereditary spherocytosis has recurrent symptomatic anaemia despite folic acid supplementation, with Hb consistently 7–8 g/dL. Osmotic fragility test is positive. What definitive treatment should be considered?','["A) Splenectomy", "B) Regular packed red cell transfusions every 4 weeks", "C) Prednisolone 1 mg/kg/day", "D) Rituximab infusion"]','A','Splenectomy is the curative treatment for hereditary spherocytosis as the spleen is the primary site of spherocyte destruction. It is indicated in moderate-to-severe disease (recurrent symptomatic anaemia, growth failure, or aplastic crises). Post-splenectomy, patients need prophylactic penicillin V, pneumococcal, meningococcal, and Hib vaccinations due to risk of overwhelming post-splenectomy infection.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Haemolytic Anaemia — Clinical Management','hard','mcq','A 35-year-old woman presents with severe headache, confusion, purpura, Hb 7.1 g/dL, platelet count 18 × 10⁹/L, and creatinine 210 µmol/L. Blood film shows numerous schistocytes. What is the most likely diagnosis and urgent treatment?','["A) Immune thrombocytopenic purpura \u2014 start IVIG", "B) Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura \u2014 start plasma exchange urgently", "C) DIC \u2014 give fresh frozen plasma and cryoprecipitate", "D) Haemolytic uraemic syndrome \u2014 start haemodialysis"]','B','This presentation is classic for thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP): the pentad of microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia (schistocytes), thrombocytopenia, neurological features, fever, and renal impairment. TTP is a medical emergency requiring urgent therapeutic plasma exchange (plasmapheresis) to remove ultra-large vWF multimers and replenish ADAMTS13. Delay increases mortality; FFP is a temporising measure only.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Haemolytic Anaemia — Clinical Management','easy','mcq','A patient with cold agglutinin disease has Hb 8.8 g/dL and worsening acrocyanosis in winter. Which immediate management is most important?','["A) Commence hydroxyurea 500 mg daily", "B) Start prednisolone 1 mg/kg/day", "C) Avoid cold exposure and keep extremities warm", "D) Request urgent splenectomy referral"]','C','Cold agglutinin disease is caused by IgM antibodies that activate complement and cause haemolysis at cold temperatures. The primary management includes strict avoidance of cold exposure (including cold environments, cold drinks, and cold IV fluids). Steroids are largely ineffective in cold AIHA. Rituximab is used for significant disease. Splenectomy is not beneficial.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Haemolytic Anaemia — Clinical Management','medium','mcq','A 22-year-old woman with warm AIHA is on prednisolone 60 mg/day but her Hb remains 7.2 g/dL after 4 weeks. She has significant Cushingoid side effects. What is the most appropriate next step?','["A) Increase prednisolone to 100 mg/day", "B) Commence azathioprine and continue prednisolone at same dose", "C) Transfuse packed red cells and stop prednisolone", "D) Proceed to splenectomy or consider rituximab"]','D','Steroid-refractory AIHA (failure to respond after 3–4 weeks of adequate steroid therapy) warrants second-line treatment. Splenectomy offers durable remission in ~60–70% of warm AIHA cases. Rituximab (anti-CD20) is an alternative for patients unfit for surgery or those who decline it. Simply increasing steroid dose increases toxicity without clear benefit.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Haemolytic Anaemia — Clinical Management','hard','mcq','A 30-year-old woman with known G6PD deficiency presents with acute haemolysis (Hb 6.0 g/dL) after nitrofurantoin was prescribed for a urinary tract infection. Which statement about G6PD deficiency is most accurate?','["A) G6PD deficiency is X-linked recessive; haemolysis is triggered by oxidant drugs, infections, and fava beans", "B) G6PD deficiency causes chronic lifelong haemolytic anaemia similar to hereditary spherocytosis", "C) Heinz bodies are not found in G6PD deficiency and their presence excludes this diagnosis", "D) G6PD deficiency is autosomal dominant and affects males and females equally"]','A','G6PD deficiency is an X-linked recessive condition, primarily affecting males (females can be affected if homozygous or have extreme lyonisation). It causes episodic acute haemolysis triggered by oxidant stress (infections, drugs such as primaquine, dapsone, nitrofurantoin, and fava beans) rather than chronic haemolysis. Blood film shows Heinz bodies (denatured Hb) and bite cells. Between episodes the blood count is typically normal.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Haemolytic Anaemia — Clinical Management','medium','mcq','A 40-year-old woman with chronic haemolytic anaemia is to be supplemented long-term. Which supplement is routinely recommended in all chronic haemolytic states?','["A) Iron supplementation to replace lost iron from haemolysis", "B) Folic acid 5 mg daily to support increased erythropoiesis", "C) Vitamin C 1 g daily to prevent oxidative damage", "D) Vitamin B12 1 mg IM every 3 months"]','B','In chronic haemolytic anaemia, the markedly increased erythropoiesis (to compensate for ongoing RBC destruction) leads to high folate consumption. Folic acid 5 mg daily is routinely supplemented to prevent folate deficiency and megaloblastic change superimposed on haemolytic anaemia. Iron is not routinely supplemented as intravascular haemolysis can cause iron overload (except if iron deficiency is confirmed).','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Sickle Cell Disease — Clinical Management','medium','mcq','A 24-year-old man with known sickle cell disease presents with severe bilateral leg pain, rated 9/10 in severity. He has no fever or signs of infection. What is the first-line analgesic approach for a moderate-to-severe vaso-occlusive crisis?','["A) Pethidine IM, as it is the most effective opioid in sickle cell pain", "B) Codeine phosphate 30 mg 6 hourly as the preferred opioid", "C) Paracetamol and NSAIDs first; escalate to morphine for severe uncontrolled pain", "D) Immediate morphine infusion without trying non-opioid analgesia first"]','C','Management of vaso-occlusive crisis follows an analgesic ladder: paracetamol (500 mg–1 g 6–8 hourly) and/or NSAIDs (ibuprofen 400 mg) for mild-moderate pain, escalating to tramadol or codeine, then to morphine under specialist supervision for severe pain. Pethidine (meperidine) is no longer recommended due to accumulation of its metabolite norpethidine, which causes seizures. Adequate hydration and oxygen should accompany analgesia.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Sickle Cell Disease — Clinical Management','hard','mcq','A 19-year-old woman with sickle cell disease presents with sudden onset right-sided hemiplegia, facial droop, and dysarthria. Hb is 6.2 g/dL. CT head shows no haemorrhage. What is the most appropriate immediate intervention?','["A) Start hydroxyurea and transfer to neurology ward", "B) Give packed red cell transfusion to raise Hb to > 12 g/dL", "C) Start aspirin 300 mg stat and thrombolysis with alteplase", "D) Perform exchange transfusion urgently to reduce HbS to < 30%"]','D','Acute ischaemic stroke in sickle cell disease requires urgent exchange transfusion (not simple transfusion) to rapidly reduce HbS levels to below 30% while preventing hyperviscosity. Simple top-up transfusion raises the total Hb and viscosity without sufficiently diluting HbS. Thrombolytics are relatively contraindicated. Exchange transfusion is the treatment of choice and must be initiated as soon as possible.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Sickle Cell Disease — Clinical Management','medium','mcq','A 32-year-old man with sickle cell disease is admitted with a vaso-occlusive crisis. In addition to analgesia, what IV fluid is recommended for hydration per Ghana STG?','["A) IV 5% dextrose in 0.9% NaCl at 2\u20134 L per day", "B) 5% dextrose in water alone, 1 L over 24 hours", "C) 0.9% normal saline, 4 L over 24 hours", "D) Hartmann''s solution at 125 mL/hour for 48 hours"]','A','The Ghana STG recommends hydration with IV 5% dextrose in 0.9% NaCl at 2–4 L per day during a sickle cell crisis to reduce blood viscosity, prevent sickling, and maintain renal perfusion. Pure 0.9% saline can worsen acidosis which promotes sickling. Adequate hydration is a cornerstone of crisis management alongside analgesia and oxygen.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Sickle Cell Disease — Clinical Management','easy','mcq','A 6-year-old boy with sickle cell disease attends for routine review. Which two preventive medications should he be on?','["A) Hydroxyurea and aspirin daily", "B) Folic acid 5 mg daily and prophylactic penicillin V", "C) Prophylactic chloroquine and folic acid", "D) Iron supplements and folic acid"]','B','Children with sickle cell disease have functional hyposplenism from early childhood, making them susceptible to encapsulated organisms (Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, Neisseria meningitidis). Prophylactic penicillin V (125 mg twice daily under 5 years; 250 mg twice daily thereafter) is recommended. Folic acid 5 mg daily compensates for increased folate consumption due to chronic haemolysis and high erythroid turnover.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Sickle Cell Disease — Clinical Management','hard','mcq','A 28-year-old man with sickle cell disease develops fever (38.8°C), pleuritic chest pain, and a new pulmonary infiltrate on CXR. Oxygen saturation is 91% on air. What is this complication and how should it be managed?','["A) Pulmonary embolism \u2014 start therapeutic anticoagulation with heparin", "B) Community-acquired pneumonia \u2014 start amoxicillin and review in 48 hours", "C) Acute chest syndrome \u2014 give oxygen, analgesia, antibiotics, and consider exchange transfusion", "D) Sickle cell lung disease \u2014 start long-term sildenafil"]','C','Acute chest syndrome (ACS) is a life-threatening complication of SCD defined by a new pulmonary infiltrate plus fever, chest pain, tachypnoea, or hypoxia. Management includes oxygen (target SpO2 >95%), analgesia (to encourage deep breathing), empirical broad-spectrum antibiotics (to cover atypical organisms), bronchodilators, and urgent blood transfusion or exchange transfusion for severe or deteriorating cases. ACS has a mortality of up to 10% and requires urgent specialist care.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Sickle Cell Disease — Clinical Management','medium','mcq','A 35-year-old woman with sickle cell disease has been having 4–5 vaso-occlusive crises per year requiring hospitalisation. Her haematologist considers disease-modifying therapy. Which drug reduces crisis frequency by increasing fetal haemoglobin (HbF)?','["A) Prednisolone", "B) Crizanlizumab", "C) Azathioprine", "D) Hydroxyurea (hydroxycarbamide)"]','D','Hydroxyurea (hydroxycarbamide) is the most widely used disease-modifying agent in sickle cell disease. It increases fetal haemoglobin (HbF) production by reactivating the gamma-globin gene, diluting the proportion of HbS and reducing sickling. It also reduces leucocytosis and improves red cell hydration. It decreases crisis frequency, acute chest syndrome episodes, transfusion requirements, and mortality. It is prescribed under specialist supervision.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Sickle Cell Disease — Clinical Management','easy','mcq','At what haemoglobin level does the Ghana STG recommend blood transfusion in sickle cell disease?','["A) Hb < 5 g/dL", "B) Hb < 7 g/dL with no symptoms", "C) Hb < 8 g/dL in any patient", "D) Hb < 10 g/dL during crisis"]','A','The Ghana STG recommends packed red cell transfusion in sickle cell disease when Hb falls below 5 g/dL, or at higher levels if there is evidence of haemodynamic compromise, aplastic crisis, splenic sequestration, or acute chest syndrome. Routine transfusion at higher haemoglobin levels risks hyperviscosity and alloimmunisation. Target post-transfusion Hb is approximately 10 g/dL.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Sickle Cell Disease — Clinical Management','medium','mcq','A 5-year-old boy with sickle cell disease presents with sudden, severe left-sided abdominal pain, a rapidly enlarging spleen, and Hb drop from 9 g/dL to 4.5 g/dL over 6 hours. What is the diagnosis?','["A) Acute vaso-occlusive crisis", "B) Acute splenic sequestration crisis", "C) Hepatic sequestration", "D) Aplastic crisis"]','B','Acute splenic sequestration crisis occurs predominantly in young children with SCD (before auto-infarction of the spleen). Large volumes of blood pool suddenly in the spleen causing rapid splenomegaly, acute anaemia (Hb drop >2 g/dL from baseline), and hypovolaemic shock. It carries a high mortality if not treated promptly with blood transfusion. Parents are taught to palpate the spleen at home. Aplastic crisis causes anaemia without splenomegaly.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Sickle Cell Disease — Clinical Management','hard','mcq','Why is pethidine (meperidine) no longer recommended for pain management in sickle cell vaso-occlusive crisis?','["A) Pethidine causes sickling of red blood cells by reducing blood pH", "B) Pethidine is less potent than paracetamol for sickle cell pain", "C) Pethidine''s metabolite norpethidine accumulates and causes seizures, particularly with repeated dosing", "D) Pethidine is contraindicated in all haematological conditions"]','C','Norpethidine is an active toxic metabolite of pethidine that accumulates with repeated dosing, particularly in renal impairment (common in SCD), and causes central nervous system toxicity including tremors, myoclonus, and seizures. Current guidelines including Ghana STG and NICE recommend morphine as the preferred strong opioid for severe sickle cell pain. Pethidine should not be used in sickle cell disease management.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Sickle Cell Disease — Clinical Management','medium','mcq','A 30-year-old man with sickle cell disease visits the travel medicine clinic before a trip abroad. Which vaccine is most critical given his splenic dysfunction?','["A) Hepatitis A vaccine", "B) Yellow fever vaccine", "C) Typhoid oral vaccine", "D) Pneumococcal vaccine (PCV13 and PPSV23)"]','D','Patients with sickle cell disease have functional asplenia from repeated splenic infarctions, making them highly susceptible to overwhelming infection by encapsulated organisms — particularly Streptococcus pneumoniae. Pneumococcal vaccination (both conjugate PCV13 and polysaccharide PPSV23) is essential, along with meningococcal and Hib vaccines. Additionally, annual influenza vaccination is recommended. Prophylactic penicillin is given lifelong in those with functional asplenia.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thalassaemia — Clinical Management','medium','mcq','A 3-year-old boy presents with pallor, failure to thrive, and progressive abdominal distension. Hb is 5.2 g/dL, MCV 58 fL. Blood film shows severe microcytic hypochromic anaemia with target cells, nucleated red blood cells, and teardrop cells. Hb electrophoresis shows absence of HbA with markedly elevated HbF. What is the diagnosis?','["A) Beta-thalassaemia major (Cooley''s anaemia)", "B) Severe iron deficiency anaemia", "C) Alpha-thalassaemia trait", "D) Sickle cell disease"]','A','Beta-thalassaemia major presents in the first year of life with severe transfusion-dependent anaemia, splenomegaly, hepatomegaly, and bony deformities (frontal bossing, maxillary hyperplasia). The blood film shows severe microcytic changes, target cells, nucleated RBCs, and teardrop cells. Hb electrophoresis reveals absent or markedly reduced HbA with compensatory elevation of HbF. It requires lifelong regular blood transfusions.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thalassaemia — Clinical Management','hard','mcq','A 10-year-old girl with beta-thalassaemia major on regular transfusions every 4 weeks has a serum ferritin of 4,800 ng/mL. She is developing bronze skin discolouration and elevated liver enzymes. What complication has developed and how should it be treated?','["A) Alloimmunisation \u2014 give washed red cells for future transfusions", "B) Iron overload (transfusional haemosiderosis) \u2014 start iron chelation therapy", "C) Haemochromatosis from dietary iron \u2014 restrict dietary iron", "D) Hepatitis C from previous transfusions \u2014 start antiviral therapy"]','B','Each unit of packed red cells contains approximately 200–250 mg of iron, which accumulates as transfusional haemosiderosis. Iron deposits in the liver, heart, endocrine organs, and skin cause organ damage (cirrhosis, cardiomyopathy, diabetes, hypogonadism, bronze skin). Iron chelation therapy with desferrioxamine (SC infusion 20–40 mg/kg over 8–12 hours, 5–7 nights/week) or oral deferasirox is indicated when ferritin exceeds 1000 ng/mL.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thalassaemia — Clinical Management','medium','mcq','What is the target pre-transfusion Hb in a child with beta-thalassaemia major on a regular transfusion regimen?','["A) Hb > 6 g/dL pre-transfusion", "B) Hb > 12 g/dL pre-transfusion", "C) Hb > 9\u201310 g/dL pre-transfusion", "D) Hb > 7 g/dL pre-transfusion"]','C','In beta-thalassaemia major, regular transfusions every 3–4 weeks aim to maintain pre-transfusion Hb above 9–10 g/dL. This suppresses endogenous ineffective erythropoiesis, which is responsible for bony deformities, extramedullary haematopoiesis, and splenomegaly. Maintaining adequate Hb allows normal growth and development. Post-transfusion Hb should not exceed 14–15 g/dL to avoid hyperviscosity.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thalassaemia — Clinical Management','easy','mcq','A 25-year-old woman is found to have Hb 10.8 g/dL, MCV 68 fL, normal serum ferritin, and elevated HbA2 of 4.2% on Hb electrophoresis. She is asymptomatic. What is the diagnosis?','["A) Sickle cell trait", "B) Iron deficiency anaemia", "C) Beta-thalassaemia major", "D) Beta-thalassaemia minor (trait)"]','D','Beta-thalassaemia trait (minor) presents with mild microcytic anaemia with normal or near-normal Hb, normal iron studies, and elevated HbA2 >3.5% on Hb electrophoresis. Individuals are usually asymptomatic and require no treatment. The importance lies in genetic counselling — if both partners carry the trait, there is a 25% chance of beta-thalassaemia major in offspring. Iron supplementation is not indicated unless coexistent IDA is confirmed.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thalassaemia — Clinical Management','hard','mcq','A 12-year-old boy with beta-thalassaemia major has been receiving transfusions every 3 weeks. His transfusion requirement has increased from 200 mL/kg/year to 360 mL/kg/year over the last 6 months, and splenomegaly is massive. What is the most likely reason and what intervention should be considered?','["A) Hypersplenism due to massive splenomegaly \u2014 consider splenectomy", "B) Alloimmunisation to red cell antigens \u2014 provide fully phenotype-matched blood", "C) Iron overload reducing marrow function \u2014 intensify iron chelation", "D) Development of aplastic crisis \u2014 treat with corticosteroids"]','A','When transfusion requirements increase to more than 1.5× the baseline (indicating hypersplenism), splenectomy should be considered. The enlarged spleen sequesters and destroys transfused red cells, increasing the volume required to maintain target Hb. Splenectomy is deferred until after age 5 if possible due to post-splenectomy infection risk. Pre-splenectomy vaccination (pneumococcal, meningococcal, Hib) and lifelong prophylactic antibiotics are essential.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thalassaemia — Clinical Management','medium','mcq','Which treatment offers the only potential cure for beta-thalassaemia major?','["A) Desferrioxamine chelation therapy", "B) Allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation from a matched sibling donor", "C) Long-term erythropoietin injections", "D) Hydroxyurea to increase HbF production"]','B','Allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation (HSCT) from a matched sibling donor is the only curative treatment currently available for beta-thalassaemia major. It has a cure rate of 85–90% in low-risk patients (young, well-chelated, no hepatomegaly). Gene therapy is emerging but not yet standard of care. Hydroxyurea increases HbF with modest benefit in some patients but is not curative. Chelation and transfusions are lifelong management, not cures.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thalassaemia — Clinical Management','medium','mcq','A 14-year-old girl with thalassaemia major on desferrioxamine chelation develops pain and swelling at the subcutaneous infusion site and refuses night-time pump therapy. Which alternative oral chelation agent can be prescribed?','["A) Oral iron supplementation to compete with iron absorption", "B) Deferoxamine IV bolus daily", "C) Deferasirox (Exjade) orally once daily", "D) Deferiprone (L1) alone at standard dose"]','C','Deferasirox (Exjade) is an oral iron chelator taken once daily, offering a convenient alternative to subcutaneous desferrioxamine infusions for patients with compliance difficulties. It is well-tolerated but requires monitoring of renal function, liver enzymes, and hearing. Deferiprone is also oral but is often combined with desferrioxamine (combination chelation) for high iron burden. Oral iron supplementation would worsen iron overload.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thalassaemia — Clinical Management','easy','mcq','Which of the following describes the iron studies expected in a patient with beta-thalassaemia trait who does NOT have concurrent iron deficiency?','["A) Low ferritin, low serum iron, high TIBC", "B) High ferritin, low serum iron, high TIBC", "C) Low ferritin, normal serum iron, normal TIBC", "D) Normal ferritin, normal serum iron, normal TIBC"]','D','In beta-thalassaemia trait (minor) without coexistent iron deficiency, iron stores are normal — ferritin, serum iron, and TIBC are all within normal limits. The microcytic anaemia is due to impaired beta-globin synthesis, not iron deficiency. Crucially, iron should NOT be prescribed empirically for microcytic anaemia without checking iron studies, as thalassaemia trait is often misdiagnosed as IDA.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thalassaemia — Clinical Management','hard','mcq','A couple attends genetic counselling. Both partners are confirmed beta-thalassaemia carriers (trait). What is the probability of each pregnancy resulting in beta-thalassaemia major?','["A) 25% chance of beta-thalassaemia major, 50% carrier, 25% normal", "B) 100% of children will be carriers but none will have major disease", "C) 0% \u2014 carriers never produce affected offspring", "D) 50% chance of major disease in each pregnancy"]','A','When both parents carry one defective beta-globin allele (beta-thalassaemia trait), each pregnancy has a 25% chance of inheriting both defective alleles (beta-thalassaemia major), a 50% chance of being a carrier (trait), and a 25% chance of inheriting two normal alleles. This follows Mendelian autosomal recessive inheritance. Prenatal diagnosis via chorionic villus sampling or amniocentesis should be offered.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thalassaemia — Clinical Management','medium','mcq','A 16-year-old with thalassaemia major has a serum ferritin of 5,200 ng/mL despite desferrioxamine therapy at 20 mg/kg/day SC for 5 nights per week. What should be the next management step?','["A) Stop chelation and monitor ferritin every 6 months", "B) Increase desferrioxamine dose to 40 mg/kg/day for 7 nights per week, or add oral deferiprone (combination chelation)", "C) Switch to blood type-matched transfusions only \u2014 no chelation change needed", "D) Commence phlebotomy to reduce iron stores"]','B','For inadequate response to chelation (ferritin >2500 ng/mL or persistently high and not trending down), options include increasing desferrioxamine dose (up to 40 mg/kg/day, 7 nights/week), extending infusion duration, or adding oral deferiprone (combination chelation), which has synergistic effects. Phlebotomy is contraindicated in thalassaemia major as it would worsen anaemia. The target ferritin is <1000 ng/mL.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Acute Leukaemia — Clinical Approach','hard','mcq','A 55-year-old man presents with a 3-week history of fatigue, spontaneous gum bleeding, and recurrent infections. FBC shows Hb 7.8 g/dL, WBC 48 × 10⁹/L, platelets 22 × 10⁹/L. Blood film shows 35% blasts with Auer rods. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A) Chronic myeloid leukaemia in blast crisis", "B) Myelodysplastic syndrome", "C) Acute myeloid leukaemia (AML)", "D) Acute lymphoblastic leukaemia (ALL)"]','C','Acute myeloid leukaemia is the most likely diagnosis given the adult patient, pancytopenia with circulating blasts, and Auer rods on blood film. Auer rods are pathognomonic of AML — they are needle-like crystalline inclusions found in myeloid blasts and are never seen in ALL. Diagnosis requires >20% blasts in peripheral blood or bone marrow (WHO 2016 criteria). Bone marrow biopsy with cytogenetics and immunophenotyping is required for classification.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Acute Leukaemia — Clinical Approach','medium','mcq','A 6-year-old boy presents with bone pain, pallor, lymphadenopathy, and hepatosplenomegaly. FBC shows Hb 8.1 g/dL, WBC 62 × 10⁹/L, platelets 35 × 10⁹/L with circulating lymphoblasts. Which leukaemia is most common in this age group?','["A) Chronic lymphocytic leukaemia (CLL)", "B) Acute myeloid leukaemia (AML)", "C) Juvenile myelomonocytic leukaemia (JMML)", "D) Acute lymphoblastic leukaemia (ALL)"]','D','Acute lymphoblastic leukaemia (ALL) is the most common childhood malignancy and the most common leukaemia in children, with peak incidence at age 2–5 years. It presents with symptoms of marrow failure (anaemia, thrombocytopenia, neutropenia), lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly, and bone pain. Lymphoblasts are TdT-positive and express B-cell or T-cell markers. The overall cure rate in children is approximately 90% with modern chemotherapy.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Acute Leukaemia — Clinical Approach','medium','mcq','A 45-year-old woman with AML on induction chemotherapy develops a temperature of 38.6°C and absolute neutrophil count of 0.3 × 10⁹/L. She appears unwell. What is the most critical immediate intervention?','["A) Start broad-spectrum IV antibiotics within 1 hour of presentation", "B) Repeat blood cultures and await results before starting antibiotics", "C) Commence oral co-amoxiclav and review in 24 hours", "D) Give G-CSF and withhold antibiotics until temperature reaches 39\u00b0C"]','A','Febrile neutropenia (temperature ≥38.3°C or ≥38°C sustained for 1 hour, with ANC <0.5 × 10⁹/L) is a medical emergency in oncology patients. Broad-spectrum IV antibiotics must be started within 1 hour of presentation — before awaiting blood culture results — as septic shock can develop rapidly. First-line regimens include piperacillin-tazobactam (pip-tazo) or ceftazidime. Delay in antibiotic therapy significantly increases mortality.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Acute Leukaemia — Clinical Approach','easy','mcq','Which finding on peripheral blood film is pathognomonic of acute myeloid leukaemia (AML)?','["A) Hypersegmented neutrophils", "B) Auer rods in myeloid blasts", "C) Teardrop cells (dacrocytes)", "D) Howell-Jolly bodies"]','B','Auer rods are elongated, needle-shaped crystalline inclusions found in the cytoplasm of myeloid blasts. They are formed from fused primary granules and are pathognomonic of AML — their presence on blood film or bone marrow aspirate is diagnostic of myeloid rather than lymphoid lineage. They are particularly prominent in acute promyelocytic leukaemia (APL/AML-M3), where bundles of Auer rods ("faggot cells") are characteristic.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Acute Leukaemia — Clinical Approach','medium','mcq','A 52-year-old woman with newly diagnosed AML has a WBC of 95 × 10⁹/L with 80% blasts. She develops sudden dyspnoea and confusion. Which life-threatening early complication of AML is most likely?','["A) Pulmonary embolism from deep vein thrombosis", "B) Intracranial haemorrhage from hypertension", "C) Hyperleukocytosis with leucostasis", "D) Acute respiratory distress syndrome from infection"]','C','Hyperleukocytosis (WBC >100 × 10⁹/L) in AML can cause leucostasis — the sludging of blasts in microvessels of the lungs and brain causing respiratory failure and altered consciousness. This is a haematological emergency managed with urgent cytoreduction (hydroxyurea, leukapheresis) to rapidly lower the WBC, along with supportive care. Platelet transfusions may be needed if thrombocytopenic bleeding occurs. Allopurinol or rasburicase is given to prevent tumour lysis syndrome.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Acute Leukaemia — Clinical Approach','medium','mcq','A 28-year-old woman with AML develops acute confusion, bleeding from multiple sites, and laboratory results show PT 24 seconds, APTT 58 seconds, fibrinogen 0.8 g/L, D-dimer markedly elevated. Blood film shows schistocytes. Bone marrow cytogenetics reveal t(15;17). What is the diagnosis and specific treatment?','["A) Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia — stop heparin and start argatroban", "B) Idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura — give IVIG and prednisolone", "C) Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura — start plasma exchange urgently", "D) Acute promyelocytic leukaemia (APL) with DIC — start ATRA immediately"]','D','Acute promyelocytic leukaemia (APL, AML-M3) is caused by the t(15;17) translocation generating the PML-RARα fusion protein. It classically presents with DIC due to release of procoagulant granules from abnormal promyelocytes. All-trans retinoic acid (ATRA) must be started immediately upon clinical suspicion — even before cytogenetic confirmation — as it induces differentiation of the abnormal cells and rapidly corrects the coagulopathy. ATRA combined with arsenic trioxide (ATO) is now standard first-line therapy.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Acute Leukaemia — Clinical Approach','hard','mcq','A 7-year-old boy is diagnosed with ALL. After 4 weeks of induction chemotherapy he achieves complete remission. His oncologist discusses CNS prophylaxis. Which approach is currently recommended to prevent CNS relapse in ALL?','["A) Intrathecal methotrexate administered during consolidation and maintenance phases", "B) Whole-brain radiotherapy in all patients with ALL", "C) Systemic high-dose cytarabine alone without intrathecal therapy", "D) Lumbar puncture for CSF sampling only — no prophylaxis needed in remission"]','A','CNS prophylaxis is essential in ALL because the blood-brain barrier limits penetration of systemic chemotherapy, making the CNS a sanctuary site for leukaemia. Intrathecal methotrexate (with or without cytarabine and hydrocortisone) delivered via lumbar puncture during consolidation and maintenance is the current standard. Cranial radiotherapy was previously used but is now avoided in most patients due to late neurocognitive effects. High-risk patients (T-cell ALL, CNS disease at presentation) may receive more intensive intrathecal regimens.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Acute Leukaemia — Clinical Approach','medium','mcq','A 19-year-old woman is diagnosed with ALL. Cytogenetics show t(9;22) Philadelphia chromosome (BCR-ABL1 fusion). What is the prognostic significance and how does this alter management?','["A) Favourable prognosis — standard BFM chemotherapy is sufficient without additional targeted therapy", "B) Poor prognosis — add a tyrosine kinase inhibitor (imatinib or dasatinib) to chemotherapy", "C) Intermediate prognosis — increase prednisolone dose only", "D) Excellent prognosis in young adults — reduce chemotherapy intensity to minimise toxicity"]','B','The Philadelphia chromosome t(9;22) in ALL is the single most adverse cytogenetic finding, historically conferring a very poor prognosis with chemotherapy alone. The BCR-ABL1 fusion produces a constitutively active tyrosine kinase. Adding a tyrosine kinase inhibitor (imatinib or dasatinib) to standard induction chemotherapy dramatically improves response rates and overall survival. Patients are also considered for allogeneic stem cell transplantation in first remission given the high relapse risk.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Acute Leukaemia — Clinical Approach','easy','mcq','Which of the following correctly defines the blast percentage threshold for diagnosing acute myeloid leukaemia (AML) on bone marrow examination according to WHO 2016 criteria?','["A) ≥10% blasts in bone marrow", "B) ≥30% blasts in bone marrow", "C) ≥20% blasts in bone marrow or peripheral blood", "D) ≥50% blasts in bone marrow only"]','C','The WHO 2016 classification defines AML as ≥20% myeloid blasts in the bone marrow or peripheral blood. This replaced the older FAB threshold of ≥30% blasts. Notably, certain recurrent cytogenetic abnormalities (t(8;21), inv(16), t(15;17)) are classified as AML regardless of blast count. Bone marrow biopsy with immunophenotyping, cytogenetics, and molecular studies is required for full classification.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Acute Leukaemia — Clinical Approach','hard','mcq','A 62-year-old man with AML is started on induction chemotherapy. Two days later his potassium is 6.8 mmol/L, phosphate 3.2 mmol/L, urate 780 µmol/L, calcium 1.7 mmol/L, and creatinine is rising. He is oliguric. What complication has occurred and what is the priority treatment?','["A) Hypercalcaemia of malignancy — give IV saline and zoledronic acid", "B) Syndrome of inappropriate ADH (SIADH) — fluid restrict to 1 litre/day", "C) Cisplatin nephrotoxicity — stop chemotherapy and hydrate", "D) Tumour lysis syndrome — aggressive IV hydration, rasburicase, manage electrolytes, renal support if needed"]','D','Tumour lysis syndrome (TLS) results from massive release of intracellular contents following rapid lysis of malignant cells, causing hyperuricaemia, hyperkalaemia, hyperphosphataemia, and hypocalcaemia (secondary to phosphate binding). This can cause acute kidney injury, cardiac arrhythmias, and seizures. Management includes aggressive IV hydration, rasburicase (urate oxidase) to lower uric acid rapidly, electrolyte correction (treat hyperkalaemia urgently), and renal replacement therapy if AKI is severe. Allopurinol is used for prophylaxis before chemotherapy.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Lymphoma — Diagnosis & Staging','easy','mcq','A 25-year-old man presents with painless cervical lymphadenopathy, fever >38°C, drenching night sweats, and 12% body weight loss over 5 months. CT scan shows mediastinal and para-aortic nodal disease. Lymph node biopsy shows Reed-Sternberg cells (CD30+, CD15+). What is the diagnosis?','["A) Classical Hodgkin lymphoma", "B) Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL)", "C) Burkitt lymphoma", "D) Peripheral T-cell lymphoma"]','A','Classical Hodgkin lymphoma is characterised by Reed-Sternberg cells (large binucleated cells with prominent owl-eye nucleoli) expressing CD30 and CD15, set in an inflammatory background. It has a bimodal age distribution (peak at 15–35 years and >55 years). B symptoms (fever >38°C, drenching night sweats, >10% weight loss in 6 months) indicate systemic disease and confer a worse prognosis. Most patients present with mediastinal adenopathy. Cure rates exceed 80–90% with ABVD chemotherapy.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Lymphoma — Diagnosis & Staging','medium','mcq','A 30-year-old woman has biopsy-confirmed Hodgkin lymphoma. PET-CT shows involvement of cervical and axillary nodes on the left side only. There is no extranodal disease. What is her Ann Arbor stage?','["A) Stage I", "B) Stage II", "C) Stage III", "D) Stage IV"]','B','Ann Arbor staging for lymphoma: Stage I = single lymph node region or single extranodal site; Stage II = two or more lymph node regions on the same side of the diaphragm; Stage III = lymph node regions on both sides of the diaphragm; Stage IV = diffuse extranodal involvement. This patient has cervical and axillary disease — two node regions, both above the diaphragm — making this Stage II. The suffix ''''B'''' is added if B symptoms are present.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Lymphoma — Diagnosis & Staging','medium','mcq','A 40-year-old man with stage IIIA Hodgkin lymphoma is commenced on chemotherapy. Which regimen is the standard first-line treatment for Hodgkin lymphoma?','["A) R-CHOP (rituximab, cyclophosphamide, doxorubicin, vincristine, prednisolone)", "B) BFM (Berlin-Frankfurt-Münster) protocol with methotrexate", "C) ABVD (doxorubicin, bleomycin, vinblastine, dacarbazine)", "D) CHOP alone without rituximab"]','C','ABVD (doxorubicin/Adriamycin, bleomycin, vinblastine, dacarbazine) is the standard first-line chemotherapy regimen for classical Hodgkin lymphoma. It achieves complete remission in over 70% of patients with advanced disease and approximately 90% in early-stage disease. Rituximab (anti-CD20) is not used in Hodgkin lymphoma because the Reed-Sternberg cells do not typically express CD20. R-CHOP is the standard for CD20-positive Non-Hodgkin lymphomas such as DLBCL.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Lymphoma — Diagnosis & Staging','hard','mcq','A 55-year-old woman presents with rapidly enlarging cervical lymph nodes over 3 weeks, fever, and a serum LDH of 3× upper limit of normal. Biopsy shows centroblasts with high mitotic rate, Ki-67 >90%, B-cell markers (CD20+), and c-MYC rearrangement with BCL-2 co-expression. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A) Follicular lymphoma (grade 1)", "B) Marginal zone lymphoma", "C) Hodgkin lymphoma — nodular sclerosis subtype", "D) Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL) with double-hit features"]','D','Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL) is the most common aggressive NHL, presenting with rapidly enlarging nodal masses and elevated LDH. The high Ki-67 (proliferation index >90%), centroblastic morphology, and CD20 positivity confirm DLBCL. Co-occurring c-MYC and BCL-2 (or BCL-6) rearrangements define a ''''double-hit'''' or ''''triple-hit'''' lymphoma, classified as high-grade B-cell lymphoma with MYC and BCL-2/BCL-6 rearrangements — conferring a particularly poor prognosis. Intensive regimens (DA-EPOCH-R) are used.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Lymphoma — Diagnosis & Staging','medium','mcq','A 7-year-old Nigerian boy presents with a rapidly growing jaw mass over 3 weeks. He also has abdominal distension. Biopsy shows uniform intermediate-sized lymphoid cells with numerous macrophages containing cellular debris, giving a ''''starry sky'''' appearance. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A) Endemic Burkitt lymphoma", "B) Hodgkin lymphoma — mixed cellularity", "C) Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma", "D) T-lymphoblastic lymphoma"]','A','Endemic Burkitt lymphoma affects children aged 4–7 years in sub-Saharan Africa and is strongly associated with Epstein-Barr virus (EBV). It most commonly presents as a jaw or facial bone tumour, with frequent abdominal involvement. The ''''starry sky'''' pattern on histology reflects benign macrophages engulfing apoptotic tumour cells against a background of rapidly proliferating lymphoma cells (Ki-67 nearly 100%). The hallmark translocation is t(8;14) causing c-MYC overexpression. Treatment is high-intensity chemotherapy (CODOX-M/IVAC).','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Lymphoma — Diagnosis & Staging','easy','mcq','Which investigation is the gold standard for staging lymphoma and assessing treatment response?','["A) CT of chest, abdomen, and pelvis alone", "B) PET-CT (18F-FDG positron emission tomography combined with CT)", "C) MRI of the whole body", "D) Bone marrow biopsy alone"]','B','PET-CT combining 18F-FDG (fluorodeoxyglucose) uptake with CT anatomy is the gold standard for staging FDG-avid lymphomas (Hodgkin lymphoma and most aggressive NHLs) and for assessing treatment response using the Deauville score. PET-CT detects occult nodal disease and extranodal sites missed by CT alone, upstaging approximately 20% of patients. Mid-treatment PET response guides therapy escalation or de-escalation. Bone marrow biopsy may be omitted for Hodgkin lymphoma when PET-CT is performed.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Lymphoma — Diagnosis & Staging','hard','mcq','A 48-year-old HIV-positive man (CD4 count 45 cells/µL) presents with weight loss, night sweats, and rapidly growing cervical lymphadenopathy. Biopsy confirms CD20-positive aggressive B-cell lymphoma. Which additional pathogen-related diagnosis must be excluded given his immunosuppression?','["A) HTLV-1 associated adult T-cell leukaemia/lymphoma", "B) EBV-associated post-transplant lymphoproliferative disorder", "C) Primary effusion lymphoma or HHV-8-associated lymphoma (linked to Kaposi sarcoma herpesvirus)", "D) Helicobacter pylori-associated gastric MALT lymphoma"]','C','HIV-associated lymphomas include aggressive B-cell NHLs (DLBCL, Burkitt lymphoma), primary CNS lymphoma, and primary effusion lymphoma (PEL). PEL is associated with HHV-8 (Kaposi sarcoma herpesvirus) and presents as malignant effusions without nodal masses, most commonly in severely immunosuppressed patients. EBV co-infection is common in HIV-associated lymphomas. Management requires antiretroviral therapy alongside lymphoma chemotherapy. HTLV-1 causes adult T-cell leukaemia/lymphoma and is endemic in Japan and the Caribbean.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Lymphoma — Diagnosis & Staging','medium','mcq','A 65-year-old man with DLBCL (stage IV, IPI score 4) achieves complete remission after 6 cycles of R-CHOP confirmed on end-of-treatment PET-CT. Two years later he re-presents with rapidly enlarging lymphadenopathy and biopsy confirms DLBCL relapse. What is the treatment strategy?','["A) Repeat 6 cycles of R-CHOP — same regimen as first-line", "B) Best supportive care only — relapsed DLBCL is not treatable", "C) Lenalidomide monotherapy as per follicular lymphoma protocol", "D) Salvage chemotherapy (R-ICE or R-DHAP), then autologous stem cell transplantation if chemosensitive"]','D','Relapsed/refractory DLBCL in transplant-eligible patients is treated with platinum-based salvage chemotherapy — R-ICE (rituximab, ifosfamide, carboplatin, etoposide) or R-DHAP (rituximab, dexamethasone, cytarabine, cisplatin) — to achieve second complete remission. Responding patients then undergo high-dose chemotherapy followed by autologous stem cell transplantation (ASCT), which offers potential cure. CAR-T cell therapy (e.g., axicabtagene ciloleucel) is now an option for relapse after two prior lines of therapy.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Lymphoma — Diagnosis & Staging','easy','mcq','What is the preferred biopsy technique for diagnosing lymphoma in a patient with accessible lymphadenopathy?','["A) Excision biopsy of the largest accessible lymph node", "B) Fine needle aspiration (FNA) of the lymph node", "C) Core needle biopsy under ultrasound guidance is always preferred over excision", "D) Bone marrow biopsy alone is sufficient for diagnosis"]','A','Excision biopsy (surgical removal of the whole lymph node) is the preferred diagnostic technique for lymphoma because it provides adequate tissue for histological architecture, immunophenotyping, cytogenetics, and molecular studies. FNA provides insufficient material for reliable lymphoma subtyping and should be avoided. Core biopsy may be acceptable when excision is not feasible (e.g., deep nodes). The largest, most accessible node is chosen — typically avoiding inguinal nodes where reactive changes are common.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Lymphoma — Diagnosis & Staging','medium','mcq','A 50-year-old woman with follicular lymphoma (grade 1, stage IVA) has been on watchful waiting for 2 years. She now develops rapid nodal enlargement, fever, night sweats, and her LDH has tripled. Repeat biopsy shows transformed histology. What has occurred?','["A) Disease progression within the same indolent grade — increase surveillance interval", "B) Richters transformation to aggressive B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL)", "C) Development of a second primary lymphoma unrelated to follicular lymphoma", "D) Spontaneous regression followed by pseudoprogression"]','B','Transformation of follicular lymphoma to diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL) — sometimes called Richter''''s transformation when it occurs in CLL — occurs in approximately 3% of follicular lymphoma patients per year. It is heralded by rapid clinical deterioration, rising LDH, systemic B symptoms, and FDG-avid lesions on PET-CT. Biopsy confirmation is essential. Treatment requires aggressive R-CHOP-based therapy followed by ASCT in eligible patients. Prognosis is significantly worse than de novo DLBCL.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Myeloma — Diagnosis & Management','easy','mcq','A 67-year-old man presents with bone pain, fatigue, and recurrent infections. Investigations reveal Hb 9.1 g/dL, calcium 2.92 mmol/L, creatinine 198 µmol/L, total protein markedly elevated, and a paraprotein band on serum electrophoresis. Urine dipstick is negative for protein but urine electrophoresis shows Bence-Jones protein. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A) Metastatic prostate cancer to bone", "B) Primary hyperparathyroidism with renal failure", "C) Multiple myeloma", "D) Waldenströms macroglobulinaemia"]','C','Multiple myeloma is a plasma cell malignancy presenting with the CRAB criteria: hyperCalcaemia (>2.75 mmol/L), Renal impairment, Anaemia (Hb <10 g/dL), and Bone lesions. Serum electrophoresis shows an M-protein paraprotein band, and Bence-Jones protein (free light chains) in urine indicates light-chain excretion. The combination of bone pain, anaemia, renal failure, hypercalcaemia, and paraprotein is classic for myeloma. Bone marrow biopsy showing >10% clonal plasma cells confirms the diagnosis.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Myeloma — Diagnosis & Management','medium','mcq','A 70-year-old woman with newly diagnosed myeloma has hypercalcaemia (calcium 3.2 mmol/L), nausea, and confusion. She is not eligible for autologous SCT. What is the most appropriate immediate management of her hypercalcaemia?','["A) Oral calcium restriction and furosemide only", "B) Prednisolone alone to suppress plasma cell proliferation", "C) Urgent haemodialysis for calcium removal", "D) IV 0.9% saline rehydration plus intravenous zoledronic acid (bisphosphonate)"]','D','Myeloma-associated hypercalcaemia is caused by osteoclast activation by myeloma cells releasing RANKL and other cytokines. Immediate treatment is IV 0.9% saline rehydration (2–3 L over 24 hours) to correct volume depletion and promote calciuresis. IV zoledronic acid (a potent bisphosphonate) inhibits osteoclast activity and is administered once renal function permits. Bisphosphonates also reduce skeletal events in myeloma. Loop diuretics (furosemide) are only used after adequate rehydration if needed.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Myeloma — Diagnosis & Management','medium','mcq','A 58-year-old man with myeloma has serum creatinine of 320 µmol/L. His oncologist plans systemic therapy. Which myeloma drug is preferred in the setting of significant renal impairment?','["A) Bortezomib (a proteasome inhibitor) — no dose adjustment required for renal impairment", "B) Melphalan at full dose — renal clearance is not significant", "C) Lenalidomide at full dose — renal function does not affect dosing", "D) Thalidomide — safest in severe renal failure without monitoring"]','A','Bortezomib (a proteasome inhibitor) is the preferred agent in myeloma patients with renal impairment because it is primarily metabolised hepatically and does not require dose adjustment for renal dysfunction. It has also been shown to improve renal function in some patients. In contrast, lenalidomide is renally cleared and requires significant dose reduction or avoidance in severe renal failure. Melphalan is also renally excreted and requires dose reduction. NSAIDs and IV contrast should be strictly avoided to protect remaining renal function.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Myeloma — Diagnosis & Management','hard','mcq','A 55-year-old woman with myeloma is eligible for autologous stem cell transplantation (ASCT). After 4 cycles of induction therapy with RVD (lenalidomide, bortezomib, dexamethasone) she achieves a very good partial response. What is the role of ASCT in her management?','["A) ASCT is used as rescue therapy only after first relapse — it is not used in first remission", "B) ASCT is performed as consolidation after induction to deepen remission and prolong progression-free survival", "C) ASCT replaces the need for any further chemotherapy or maintenance", "D) ASCT is only indicated in patients with complete remission — partial response precludes transplantation"]','B','Autologous stem cell transplantation (ASCT) after high-dose melphalan conditioning is performed as consolidation in transplant-eligible myeloma patients (typically age <70 with adequate organ function) following induction chemotherapy. It deepens the response achieved with induction, prolongs progression-free survival, and improves overall survival compared to chemotherapy alone. ASCT does not cure myeloma but significantly extends remission duration. Post-ASCT lenalidomide maintenance further prolongs progression-free survival.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Myeloma — Diagnosis & Management','medium','mcq','Which skeletal imaging modality is now preferred over conventional plain X-ray skeletal survey for detecting myeloma bone disease?','["A) MRI of the spine only — detects vertebral disease exclusively", "B) Bone scintigraphy (technetium-99m bone scan) — sensitive for all bone lesions", "C) Whole-body low-dose CT (WBLDCT) — detects lytic lesions throughout the skeleton", "D) Dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry (DEXA) scan — measures bone mineral density"]','C','Whole-body low-dose CT (WBLDCT) is now the preferred imaging modality for myeloma bone disease, replacing the conventional skeletal survey (plain X-rays). WBLDCT detects lytic lesions earlier and with greater sensitivity than plain films (which require 30–50% cortical destruction before lesions are visible). PET-CT provides additional functional information and is used for staging and response assessment. Bone scintigraphy is NOT used in myeloma — myeloma lesions are purely lytic with no osteoblastic activity, giving false-negative bone scans.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Myeloma — Diagnosis & Management','easy','mcq','A 62-year-old man with myeloma starts lenalidomide-based therapy. His haematologist prescribes concomitant thromboprophylaxis. Why is this necessary?','["A) Lenalidomide causes thrombocytopenia and spontaneous bleeding requiring anticoagulation", "B) Myeloma itself lowers fibrinogen causing a hypercoagulable state that requires heparin", "C) All cancer patients must receive anticoagulation by default regardless of drug regimen", "D) Immunomodulatory drugs (IMiDs) including lenalidomide and thalidomide significantly increase the risk of venous thromboembolism (DVT/PE)"]','D','Immunomodulatory drugs (IMiDs) — thalidomide and lenalidomide — increase the risk of venous thromboembolism (DVT and PE), particularly when combined with dexamethasone or conventional chemotherapy. The mechanism involves effects on coagulation factors and vascular endothelium. Current guidelines recommend thromboprophylaxis (aspirin for low-risk patients, LMWH or warfarin for intermediate/high-risk) throughout IMiD therapy. Risk is highest in the first few months of treatment.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Myeloma — Diagnosis & Management','hard','mcq','A 68-year-old man with myeloma has a serum paraprotein of 38 g/L and normal free light chain ratio on initial diagnosis. After 4 months of bortezomib-based therapy his paraprotein has fallen to 12 g/L. How is treatment response typically monitored in myeloma?','["A) Serial measurement of serum M-protein (paraprotein), serum free light chains (SFLC), and 24-hour urine protein electrophoresis according to IMWG response criteria", "B) PET-CT scan every 4 weeks to assess disease burden", "C) Bone marrow biopsy monthly to count plasma cells", "D) Serum LDH and CRP as the primary response biomarkers"]','A','Myeloma treatment response is monitored using International Myeloma Working Group (IMWG) criteria based on quantifiable disease markers: serum M-protein (paraprotein on electrophoresis), serum free light chains (κ/λ ratio), and 24-hour urine protein electrophoresis for urine M-protein. Response categories include complete response (CR), very good partial response (VGPR, ≥90% M-protein reduction), partial response (PR, ≥50% reduction), and minimal response. Serial bone marrow biopsy is reserved for confirming stringent complete response (sCR).','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Myeloma — Diagnosis & Management','medium','mcq','A 71-year-old woman with myeloma develops sudden severe thoracic back pain after minimal movement. Plain X-ray shows a crush fracture of T8 vertebra with no neurological deficit. How should her vertebral fracture be managed?','["A) Bed rest alone for 6–8 weeks with simple analgesia", "B) Analgesia, continue bisphosphonate therapy, and consider vertebroplasty or kyphoplasty for pain relief", "C) Emergency surgical fixation is mandatory for all myeloma vertebral fractures", "D) High-dose corticosteroids to reduce swelling around the fracture site"]','B','Vertebral compression fractures in myeloma result from lytic bone disease. Management includes adequate analgesia (paracetamol, opioids; avoid NSAIDs), continuation of bisphosphonate therapy (zoledronic acid) to reduce further skeletal events, and orthopaedic input for instability assessment. Vertebroplasty (cement injection) or balloon kyphoplasty can provide rapid and durable pain relief for painful vertebral fractures unresponsive to analgesia. Neurological compromise (cord compression) is a surgical emergency requiring urgent MRI, dexamethasone, and spinal surgery or radiotherapy.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Myeloma — Diagnosis & Management','easy','mcq','Which of the following best describes the CRAB criteria used to define symptomatic myeloma requiring treatment?','["A) Creatinine >500 µmol/L, Reticulocytosis, Arthralgia, Bone pain", "B) Cytopenia, Raised ESR, Abnormal protein electrophoresis, Bone loss", "C) Hypercalcaemia (>2.75 mmol/L), Renal impairment, Anaemia (Hb <10 g/dL), Bone lesions", "D) Coagulopathy, Renal tubular acidosis, Ascites, Bleeding tendency"]','C','The CRAB criteria define end-organ damage from myeloma that mandates treatment: hyperCalcaemia (corrected calcium >2.75 mmol/L), Renal impairment (creatinine >177 µmol/L or eGFR <40 mL/min), Anaemia (Hb <10 g/dL or >2 g/dL below lower limit of normal), and Bone lesions (lytic lesions or pathological fractures on imaging). Additional biomarkers (SLiM criteria) — >60% clonal plasma cells, SFLC ratio >100, >1 focal MRI lesion — also define myeloma requiring treatment even without CRAB features.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Myeloma — Diagnosis & Management','hard','mcq','A 74-year-old man with smouldering myeloma has 18% bone marrow plasma cells, serum M-protein of 28 g/L, and no CRAB features. PET-CT shows no active disease. What is the recommended management?','["A) Immediate VMP (bortezomib, melphalan, prednisolone) chemotherapy — treat immediately regardless of symptoms", "B) Autologous SCT within 3 months of diagnosis to prevent progression", "C) Monthly bisphosphonates and radiotherapy to all bone lesions prophylactically", "D) Active surveillance (watchful waiting) with regular monitoring every 3 months — treat only when CRAB criteria or biomarkers of malignancy develop"]','D','Smouldering (asymptomatic) myeloma does not yet meet criteria for treatment. Standard management is active surveillance with clinical review and laboratory tests every 3 months, including serum M-protein, serum free light chains, FBC, renal function, and calcium. Treatment is initiated when CRAB criteria develop or when SLiM biomarkers indicate imminent end-organ risk. The absolute annual risk of progression to symptomatic myeloma is approximately 10% per year. Lenalidomide is being studied as preventive therapy in high-risk smouldering myeloma.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thrombocytopenia — Clinical Approach','easy','mcq','A 28-year-old woman presents with a 2-week history of easy bruising and petechiae. FBC shows platelets of 18 × 10⁹/L, Hb 12.8 g/dL, and normal WBC. Peripheral blood film shows no fragmented red cells or clumps. Coagulation screen is normal. She takes no medications. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A) Immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP)", "B) Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP)", "C) Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT)", "D) Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)"]','A','Immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP) presents with isolated thrombocytopenia — low platelet count with normal haemoglobin, white cells, and coagulation — in an otherwise well patient. Antiplatelet IgG antibodies coat platelets causing premature destruction by splenic macrophages. The absence of schistocytes excludes TTP/MAHA. The normal coagulation screen excludes DIC. No heparin exposure excludes HIT. First-line treatment is oral prednisolone (1 mg/kg/day) with IVIG used for urgent cases.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thrombocytopenia — Clinical Approach','medium','mcq','A 35-year-old woman is admitted with confusion, platelets 14 × 10⁹/L, Hb 7.2 g/dL, and creatinine 280 µmol/L. Blood film shows numerous schistocytes. ADAMTS13 activity is <5% with ADAMTS13 inhibitor detected. What is the diagnosis and the single most critical treatment?','["A) DIC — give fresh frozen plasma and cryoprecipitate urgently", "B) Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP) — commence plasma exchange (PEX) immediately", "C) HUS — supportive care with dialysis; no plasma exchange needed", "D) HIT — stop heparin and start argatroban"]','B','Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP) is caused by deficiency of ADAMTS13 (a metalloprotease that cleaves ultra-large von Willebrand factor), leading to platelet microthrombi in small vessels causing microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia (MAHA), thrombocytopenia, and multi-organ damage. The pentad includes: MAHA, thrombocytopenia, neurological features, fever, and renal failure. Plasma exchange is lifesaving and must be started within hours of diagnosis. Platelet transfusion is contraindicated as it fuels the prothrombotic process. Rituximab is added for refractory cases.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thrombocytopenia — Clinical Approach','medium','mcq','A 60-year-old man with a mechanical aortic valve has been on unfractionated heparin for 8 days post-valve surgery. His platelets have fallen from 250 × 10⁹/L to 95 × 10⁹/L. He develops a new lower limb DVT. Anti-PF4/heparin antibody test is positive. What is the diagnosis and management?','["A) Non-immune heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT type I) — continue heparin at a lower dose", "B) Heparin overdose causing haemorrhage — reverse with protamine sulphate", "C) Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia with thrombosis (HITT) — stop all heparin and start a non-heparin anticoagulant (argatroban or fondaparinux)", "D) Septic thrombophlebitis — treat with broad-spectrum antibiotics only"]','C','Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia with thrombosis (HITT) is an immune-mediated prothrombotic disorder caused by IgG antibodies against heparin-PF4 complexes, paradoxically causing platelet activation and clot formation despite thrombocytopenia. It occurs 5–10 days after heparin exposure. Management requires immediate cessation of ALL heparin products (including flushes and LMWH) and prompt initiation of a non-heparin anticoagulant — argatroban (direct thrombin inhibitor) or fondaparinux (anti-Xa). Platelet transfusion is contraindicated as it risks worsening thrombosis. Warfarin must not be started until platelet count recovers.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thrombocytopenia — Clinical Approach','hard','mcq','A 38-year-old woman with obstetric history presents with microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia, thrombocytopenia, and acute kidney injury. Unlike TTP she has severe renal impairment with minimal neurological involvement. ADAMTS13 activity is normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A) Immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP) — antiplatelet antibody mediated", "B) Catastrophic antiphospholipid syndrome (CAPS)", "C) Aplastic anaemia with secondary thrombocytopenia", "D) Haemolytic uraemic syndrome (HUS), likely atypical (aHUS) or Shiga toxin-associated"]','D','Haemolytic uraemic syndrome (HUS) presents with the triad of MAHA, thrombocytopenia, and acute kidney injury. In contrast to TTP, renal failure dominates and neurological features are less prominent. ADAMTS13 is normal in HUS. Typical HUS (STEC-HUS) follows Shiga toxin-producing E. coli O157:H7 infection (often haemorrhagic diarrhoea preceding). Atypical HUS (aHUS) results from complement dysregulation (mutations in complement regulatory proteins) and is treated with eculizumab (anti-C5 monoclonal antibody).','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thrombocytopenia — Clinical Approach','medium','mcq','A 25-year-old woman with ITP and platelet count of 8 × 10⁹/L requires an emergency appendicectomy. She has not responded to prednisolone. What is the most appropriate pre-operative management to raise her platelet count rapidly?','["A) Intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG) 1 g/kg for 1–2 days — raises platelets within 24–48 hours", "B) Rituximab 375 mg/m² — response expected within 2 weeks", "C) Splenectomy — the platelet count will normalise immediately after surgery", "D) Platelet transfusion alone — will provide adequate haemostasis for surgery"]','A','IVIG (intravenous immunoglobulin) acts by blocking Fc receptors on splenic macrophages, reducing platelet destruction and rapidly raising the platelet count within 24–48 hours. This makes it ideal for emergencies requiring rapid platelet rise before surgery or in severe bleeding. The response is transient (2–3 weeks). Rituximab takes 2–4 weeks to work. In ITP, platelet transfusions are consumed very rapidly and are generally reserved as a last resort in life-threatening bleeding — IVIG should be given first to prolong platelet survival.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thrombocytopenia — Clinical Approach','hard','mcq','A patient with severe DIC secondary to septic shock has platelets 28 × 10⁹/L, fibrinogen 0.5 g/L, PT 28 seconds, APTT 72 seconds, and raised D-dimer. She is actively bleeding from multiple sites. What blood products should be administered?','["A) Packed red cells and protamine sulphate only", "B) Fresh frozen plasma (FFP), platelet concentrate, and cryoprecipitate — plus treat underlying sepsis", "C) IVIG and rituximab for immune-mediated thrombocytopenia", "D) Heparin infusion to prevent further clot formation"]','B','DIC treatment is centred on treating the underlying cause (sepsis with antibiotics, fluids, source control) while supporting haemostasis with blood products. FFP replaces all clotting factors (PT and APTT prolongation). Cryoprecipitate provides concentrated fibrinogen (target >1.5 g/L) and factor VIII. Platelet concentrate is transfused when platelets <50 × 10⁹/L with active bleeding. Heparin is generally avoided in acute DIC with bleeding (used only in specific DIC variants such as APL with thrombosis). D-dimer reflects ongoing fibrinolysis.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thrombocytopenia — Clinical Approach','easy','mcq','At what platelet count should a prophylactic platelet transfusion be given to a patient with chemotherapy-induced thrombocytopenia who has no active bleeding and no procedure planned?','["A) Platelets < 30 × 10⁹/L", "B) Platelets < 20 × 10⁹/L", "C) Platelets < 10 × 10⁹/L", "D) Platelets < 50 × 10⁹/L"]','C','Current British Society for Haematology (BSH) guidelines recommend prophylactic platelet transfusion when the platelet count falls below 10 × 10⁹/L in stable patients with chemotherapy-induced thrombocytopenia, to prevent spontaneous haemorrhage. Thresholds are higher in specific situations: <50 × 10⁹/L before invasive procedures, <100 × 10⁹/L before neurosurgery or ophthalmic surgery, and higher thresholds for active fever or infection. Unnecessary platelet transfusions cause alloimmunisation and refractoriness.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thrombocytopenia — Clinical Approach','hard','mcq','A 55-year-old woman with chronic refractory ITP has failed prednisolone, IVIG, and rituximab. She has had two splenic episodes requiring platelet transfusions. Her spleen was removed 3 years ago (splenectomy). Platelets remain at 12 × 10⁹/L. What class of drug should now be considered?','["A) Ciclosporin — calcineurin inhibitor for T-cell suppression", "B) High-dose methylprednisolone — pulse steroids as third-line rescue", "C) Danazol — androgen with immunomodulatory properties", "D) Thrombopoietin receptor agonists (eltrombopag or romiplostim) to stimulate platelet production"]','D','Thrombopoietin receptor agonists (TPO-RAs) — eltrombopag (oral) and romiplostim (subcutaneous weekly injection) — stimulate megakaryocyte proliferation and differentiation via the TPO receptor, increasing platelet production. They are recommended for chronic refractory ITP after failure of corticosteroids, IVIG, and splenectomy. They do not address the autoimmune aetiology but maintain safe platelet counts. Platelet response occurs within 1–2 weeks. Long-term therapy may be required as platelets often fall again on discontinuation.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thrombocytopenia — Clinical Approach','medium','mcq','A 22-year-old pregnant woman at 32 weeks gestation presents with thrombocytopenia (platelets 68 × 10⁹/L), proteinuria, hypertension, and elevated liver enzymes (AST 180 U/L). She has epigastric pain. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A) HELLP syndrome (Haemolysis, Elevated Liver enzymes, Low Platelets) — a severe variant of pre-eclampsia", "B) Immune thrombocytopenic purpura — isolated thrombocytopenia of pregnancy", "C) Gestational thrombocytopenia — benign, no treatment needed", "D) TTP — treat with plasma exchange"]','A','HELLP syndrome is a life-threatening obstetric complication characterised by Haemolysis (microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia, raised LDH), Elevated Liver enzymes (hepatocellular damage, epigastric pain), and Low Platelets (<100 × 10⁹/L). It is a severe variant of pre-eclampsia and typically requires delivery, especially after 34 weeks. Blood pressure control, magnesium sulphate (seizure prophylaxis), and corticosteroids (fetal lung maturity before 34 weeks) are key. Platelet transfusion is given if <20 × 10⁹/L or before caesarean section if <50 × 10⁹/L.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thrombocytopenia — Clinical Approach','medium','mcq','A 45-year-old man with chronic ITP undergoes elective cholecystectomy. His platelet count pre-operatively is 42 × 10⁹/L. What platelet threshold is required before elective major surgery to minimise bleeding risk?','["A) ≥ 20 × 10⁹/L is sufficient for all surgeries", "B) ≥ 50 × 10⁹/L for most elective surgical procedures", "C) ≥ 150 × 10⁹/L — normal platelet count must be restored before any surgery", "D) ≥ 10 × 10⁹/L with local haemostatic measures only"]','B','BSH guidelines recommend a platelet count of ≥50 × 10⁹/L before most elective surgical procedures to minimise perioperative haemorrhage. Higher thresholds apply for neurosurgery and ophthalmic surgery (≥100 × 10⁹/L). In ITP, the platelet count can be raised acutely with IVIG (±prednisolone) before the procedure. For minor procedures such as dental extraction, a lower threshold of ≥30 × 10⁹/L may be acceptable. Platelet transfusions in ITP are consumed rapidly and should be given intraoperatively rather than pre-operatively.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anticoagulation Management','easy','mcq','A 55-year-old man is diagnosed with an unprovoked proximal DVT confirmed on Doppler ultrasound. He has no malignancy, no recent surgery, and no identifiable risk factors. What is the recommended first-line anticoagulant and duration of treatment?','["A) Aspirin 75 mg daily for 3 months — sufficient for DVT prophylaxis", "B) Warfarin for 6 weeks only — short course for unprovoked DVT", "C) A direct oral anticoagulant (DOAC) such as rivaroxaban or apixaban for a minimum of 3–6 months with consideration of long-term therapy", "D) Unfractionated heparin infusion for 3 months"]','C','For unprovoked proximal DVT, current NICE guidelines recommend anticoagulation for at least 3–6 months. DOACs (rivaroxaban or apixaban) are preferred over warfarin as first-line due to oral convenience, predictable pharmacokinetics, no routine INR monitoring, and similar efficacy and safety. After the initial treatment period, long-term anticoagulation should be considered given the high recurrence risk with unprovoked VTE (~30% at 5 years without anticoagulation), weighing bleeding risk against thrombotic risk.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anticoagulation Management','medium','mcq','A 70-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation has a CHA₂DS₂-VASc score of 4 (hypertension, age 70, diabetes, female sex). Her HASBLED score is 2. She has no contraindications to anticoagulation. What is the recommended management?','["A) Aspirin 75 mg daily — sufficient for AF stroke prevention", "B) Rate control with beta-blocker only — no anticoagulation needed with a score of 4", "C) Rhythm control with cardioversion — anticoagulation deferred until sinus rhythm restored", "D) Long-term oral anticoagulation with a DOAC (apixaban, rivaroxaban, or dabigatran) — preferred over warfarin"]','D','In non-valvular AF, CHA₂DS₂-VASc ≥2 in males (≥3 in females) indicates a high enough stroke risk to recommend anticoagulation. This patient scores 4 (1 each for hypertension, age 65–74, diabetes, and female sex) — anticoagulation is clearly indicated. DOACs (apixaban, rivaroxaban, dabigatran, edoxaban) are preferred over warfarin for non-valvular AF due to predictable dosing, no INR monitoring, and superior safety profiles. Aspirin alone is not recommended for AF stroke prevention.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anticoagulation Management','medium','mcq','A 65-year-old man with a mechanical mitral valve replacement on warfarin (target INR 2.5–3.5) requires elective total hip replacement. His INR is currently 3.1. What is the correct pre-operative anticoagulation management?','["A) Stop warfarin 5 days before surgery; provide LMWH bridging anticoagulation perioperatively; restart warfarin post-operatively with LMWH bridge until INR therapeutic", "B) Continue warfarin at the same dose throughout surgery — do not interrupt", "C) Switch to a DOAC perioperatively as DOACs are safer around surgery", "D) Stop warfarin 24 hours before surgery without bridging — INR will normalise rapidly"]','A','Patients with mechanical heart valves have a high thrombotic risk (particularly mitral valves) and require bridging anticoagulation when warfarin is interrupted. Warfarin is stopped 5 days before surgery to allow INR to fall below 1.5. Therapeutic-dose LMWH is commenced 3 days before surgery, with the last dose 24 hours pre-operatively. Post-operatively, warfarin and LMWH bridge are restarted once haemostasis is achieved, continuing LMWH until INR re-enters the therapeutic range. DOACs are absolutely contraindicated in mechanical valve patients.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anticoagulation Management','hard','mcq','A 72-year-old woman on warfarin (INR target 2.0–3.0) for AF presents to the emergency department with a sudden-onset severe headache. CT head confirms an acute intracranial haemorrhage. Her INR is 4.8. What is the most urgent haemostatic management?','["A) Oral vitamin K 10 mg and await INR re-check in 4 hours", "B) 4-factor prothrombin complex concentrate (PCC) and intravenous vitamin K 5–10 mg to achieve INR < 1.3 immediately", "C) Fresh frozen plasma (FFP) 15 mL/kg — fastest way to reverse warfarin", "D) Idarucizumab — specific reversal agent for warfarin"]','B','Warfarin-associated intracranial haemorrhage requires immediate reversal of anticoagulation to reduce haematoma expansion and mortality. 4-factor prothrombin complex concentrate (PCC — containing factors II, VII, IX, X and proteins C and S) is the preferred agent as it rapidly reverses INR within minutes, unlike FFP (which takes 30–60 minutes, requires large volumes, and risks fluid overload). IV vitamin K 5–10 mg is given simultaneously to sustain reversal. Idarucizumab is the reversal agent for dabigatran, not warfarin.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anticoagulation Management','medium','mcq','A 68-year-old man on dabigatran (150 mg twice daily) for AF is brought to the emergency department after a road traffic accident with traumatic intracranial bleeding. What is the specific reversal agent for dabigatran?','["A) Andexanet alfa — recombinant factor Xa reversal agent", "B) Protamine sulphate — reverses all anticoagulants including DOACs", "C) Idarucizumab (Praxbind) — humanised monoclonal antibody fragment that binds and neutralises dabigatran", "D) 4-factor PCC — preferred first-line for all DOAC reversal"]','C','Idarucizumab (Praxbind) is a humanised monoclonal antibody fragment that binds dabigatran with very high affinity (350 times stronger than thrombin), completely reversing anticoagulation within minutes. It is given as a fixed dose of 5 g IV (2 × 2.5 g vials). It is the specific reversal agent for dabigatran only. Andexanet alfa reverses factor Xa inhibitors (rivaroxaban and apixaban). Protamine sulphate reverses unfractionated heparin and partially reverses LMWH. 4-factor PCC is used when specific reversal agents are unavailable.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anticoagulation Management','hard','mcq','A 58-year-old woman with active metastatic colorectal cancer develops a symptomatic pulmonary embolism confirmed on CT pulmonary angiogram. What is the preferred anticoagulant in cancer-associated VTE?','["A) Warfarin with target INR 2.0–3.0 — gold standard for all VTE", "B) Standard-dose rivaroxaban for 3 months only — shorter duration in cancer", "C) Unfractionated heparin infusion for 6 months — parenteral therapy preferred in cancer", "D) Low molecular weight heparin (LMWH) such as dalteparin or enoxaparin, or DOACs with low GI bleeding risk (apixaban/rivaroxaban)"]','D','Cancer-associated VTE is associated with higher recurrence rates and bleeding complications than VTE in the general population. LMWH (dalteparin or enoxaparin) was historically preferred over warfarin due to superior efficacy and avoidance of drug-food interactions that affect INR stability in cancer patients on chemotherapy. More recent data from the SELECT-D and ADAM-VTE trials show that DOACs (rivaroxaban, apixaban) have comparable or superior efficacy in cancer-associated VTE, with the caveat of higher GI bleeding risk in GI and urothelial cancers — LMWH remains preferred in those settings.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anticoagulation Management','easy','mcq','A 45-year-old man on warfarin for recurrent VTE has an INR of 6.2 on routine monitoring. He has no signs of bleeding. What is the appropriate management?','["A) Withhold 1–2 doses of warfarin, give oral vitamin K 1–2 mg, and recheck INR in 24–48 hours", "B) Administer 4-factor PCC immediately regardless of bleeding", "C) Stop warfarin permanently and switch to a DOAC", "D) Continue warfarin at the same dose and recheck INR in 1 week"]','A','For a supratherapeutic INR >5 without bleeding, current BSH guidelines recommend withholding 1–2 warfarin doses and giving oral vitamin K 1–2 mg to facilitate INR correction. The INR should be rechecked within 24–48 hours and warfarin restarted at a lower dose once INR is back within the therapeutic range. 4-factor PCC is reserved for major or life-threatening bleeding. The underlying reason for over-anticoagulation (drug interactions, illness, dietary changes) should be investigated.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anticoagulation Management','medium','mcq','A patient receiving unfractionated heparin (UFH) infusion for treatment of acute PE has an APTT ratio of 1.1 (target 1.5–2.5). What does this indicate and what action should be taken?','["A) APTT is within target range — no change required", "B) Insufficient anticoagulation — increase heparin infusion rate according to the hospitals weight-based dosing nomogram", "C) APTT is supratherapeutic — reduce heparin infusion by 50%", "D) Switch to LMWH immediately — UFH APTT monitoring is unreliable"]','B','Unfractionated heparin is monitored with the APTT ratio, targeting 1.5–2.5× the normal control value (exact target varies by laboratory). An APTT ratio of 1.1 is sub-therapeutic, meaning the patient is not adequately anticoagulated and remains at high risk of PE extension or recurrence. The heparin infusion rate should be increased according to the hospital''''s weight-based heparin nomogram, and APTT should be rechecked 6 hours after any dose adjustment. Regular APTT monitoring is essential as heparin pharmacokinetics vary between patients.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anticoagulation Management','hard','mcq','A 35-year-old woman on LMWH (enoxaparin) for acute DVT accidentally receives an overdose. She develops haemorrhage from her IV site. What reversal agent is used for LMWH and what is its limitation?','["A) Idarucizumab — fully reverses all LMWH anticoagulant activity", "B) Oral vitamin K 10 mg — reverses LMWH within 1 hour", "C) Protamine sulphate reverses approximately 60–80% of LMWH anti-Xa activity (does not fully reverse anti-Xa activity)", "D) Andexanet alfa — not effective against LMWH, only factor Xa oral inhibitors"]','C','Protamine sulphate (1 mg per 100 units anti-Xa activity of LMWH given in the past 8 hours) partially reverses LMWH by binding to the heparin chains. However, it neutralises anti-IIa activity fully but only partially reverses anti-Xa activity (approximately 60–80% reversal), making haemostasis incomplete compared to its reversal of unfractionated heparin. Andexanet alfa (recombinant factor Xa decoy) has been shown to reverse LMWH anti-Xa activity but is not licensed for LMWH reversal in most guidelines. Vitamin K and idarucizumab have no effect on LMWH.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anticoagulation Management','medium','mcq','A 50-year-old woman had a provoked DVT (after a long-haul flight) 3 months ago and completed a full course of anticoagulation. She asks about the risk of recurrence and when anticoagulation can be safely stopped. What is the recommended duration of anticoagulation for a single provoked DVT?','["A) 6 weeks only — provoked DVT has very low recurrence risk", "B) Lifelong — all DVT requires permanent anticoagulation", "C) 12 months minimum regardless of provocation", "D) 3 months — 3 months is sufficient for a single provoked VTE with a transient reversible risk factor"]','D','A single provoked VTE (occurring in the context of a transient, reversible risk factor such as surgery, immobilisation, long-haul travel, or oral contraceptive use) carries a low recurrence risk once the provoking factor is removed. Three months of anticoagulation is the recommended minimum and is sufficient. Unprovoked VTE, cancer-associated VTE, or recurrent VTE carries a much higher recurrence risk and warrants extended or indefinite anticoagulation. Annual review is recommended for patients on long-term anticoagulation.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Blood Transfusion — Clinical Practice','easy','mcq','A 55-year-old man with Hb 6.2 g/dL has fatigue and exertional dyspnoea following a GI bleed. He is haemodynamically stable and has no cardiac history. What is the recommended haemoglobin trigger for red cell transfusion in this setting?','["A) Transfuse when Hb < 7 g/dL in haemodynamically stable patients without cardiac disease", "B) Transfuse when Hb < 10 g/dL in all patients regardless of symptoms", "C) Transfuse immediately for any Hb below normal range (13.5 g/dL in males)", "D) Only transfuse if the patient loses consciousness from anaemia"]','A','NICE guidelines (NG24) recommend a restrictive transfusion strategy with a haemoglobin threshold of 7 g/dL in haemodynamically stable patients without cardiac disease. This patient with Hb 6.2 g/dL meets the transfusion threshold. Typically 1–2 units are transfused with reassessment of Hb and symptoms after each unit. A higher threshold of 8 g/dL applies to patients with acute coronary syndrome or symptomatic cardiac disease. Symptomatic anaemia (dyspnoea, angina, postural hypotension) at any Hb level may also justify transfusion.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Blood Transfusion — Clinical Practice','medium','mcq','Thirty minutes after commencing a blood transfusion, a 42-year-old woman develops rigors, fever of 39.2°C, severe back pain, and passes dark brown urine. Her blood pressure drops to 85/50 mmHg. What is the most likely transfusion reaction and the immediate management?','["A) Febrile non-haemolytic transfusion reaction — give paracetamol and slow the transfusion rate", "B) Acute haemolytic transfusion reaction (ABO incompatibility) — STOP transfusion immediately, maintain IV access, send samples to blood bank and laboratory, monitor for DIC and renal failure", "C) TRALI — stop transfusion and commence mechanical ventilation", "D) Allergic reaction — give chlorphenamine and restart transfusion after 30 minutes"]','B','Acute haemolytic transfusion reaction due to ABO incompatibility is the most dangerous immediate transfusion reaction, typically caused by clerical error (wrong blood to wrong patient). It presents with fever, rigors, back/loin pain, haemoglobinuria (dark urine), hypotension, and DIC. The transfusion must be stopped immediately, IV access maintained with fresh IV fluids, and urgent samples sent (repeat crossmatch, FBC, coagulation screen, renal function, DAT). Monitor urine output and prevent AKI. Report to blood bank and haemovigilance team.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Blood Transfusion — Clinical Practice','medium','mcq','A 78-year-old woman with cardiac failure receives a blood transfusion. During the second unit she becomes acutely breathless with a respiratory rate of 32/min, oxygen saturation 88%, and blood pressure of 165/95 mmHg. Chest X-ray shows bilateral pulmonary oedema. JVP is elevated. What is the most likely diagnosis and management?','["A) Transfusion-Related Acute Lung Injury (TRALI) — supportive oxygen, position upright, no diuretics", "B) Anaphylaxis — adrenaline 0.5 mg IM immediately", "C) Transfusion-Associated Circulatory Overload (TACO) — stop transfusion, sit upright, give IV furosemide 40–80 mg", "D) Febrile non-haemolytic reaction — give paracetamol and restart slowly"]','C','Transfusion-Associated Circulatory Overload (TACO) occurs when the volume and rate of transfusion exceed cardiac capacity, particularly in elderly patients and those with pre-existing cardiac or renal disease. It presents with acute pulmonary oedema, hypertension, elevated JVP, and bilateral pleural effusions on chest X-ray — distinguishing it from TRALI (which presents with hypotension and non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema without hypertension). Management: stop transfusion, sit upright, administer IV furosemide, monitor closely. Future transfusions should be given slowly (4 hours per unit) with diuretic cover.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Blood Transfusion — Clinical Practice','hard','mcq','A 30-year-old woman receives her second unit of blood transfusion 4 hours after delivery. Within 2 hours she develops acute severe hypoxia (pO₂ 7.1 kPa on 15 L oxygen), bilateral infiltrates on chest X-ray, and blood pressure of 88/60 mmHg. She has no evidence of fluid overload. TRALI is suspected. What is the pathophysiology and management?','["A) IgE-mediated anaphylaxis to blood group antigens — give IM adrenaline and antihistamine", "B) ABO incompatibility — stop transfusion, give 4-factor PCC for coagulopathy", "C) Circulatory overload from rapid transfusion — give furosemide and restrict IV fluids", "D) Donor HLA antibodies activate recipient neutrophils causing non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema — stop transfusion, provide supportive oxygen/ventilatory support, report to blood bank"]','D','Transfusion-Related Acute Lung Injury (TRALI) is a leading cause of transfusion-related mortality. It occurs within 6 hours of transfusion and is characterised by acute hypoxia and bilateral pulmonary infiltrates (non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema) without evidence of circulatory overload. The pathophysiology involves donor anti-HLA or anti-neutrophil antibodies activating recipient neutrophils in pulmonary capillaries, causing capillary leak. Management is supportive: stop transfusion, high-flow oxygen, and invasive mechanical ventilation if required. All implicated blood components are reported and quarantined.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Blood Transfusion — Clinical Practice','medium','mcq','A 25-year-old woman receives a blood transfusion and develops urticaria (hives) with itching but no dyspnoea, wheeze, or haemodynamic compromise. What is the most appropriate management?','["A) Stop transfusion, give chlorphenamine (antihistamine), and restart slowly once urticaria has resolved", "B) Stop transfusion permanently and send all remaining blood products back to the blood bank for disposal", "C) Administer IM adrenaline immediately for suspected anaphylaxis", "D) Continue transfusion at a slower rate without any medication"]','A','Urticarial (allergic) transfusion reactions are common and usually mild, caused by donor plasma proteins reacting with recipient IgE. They present with urticaria, itching, and flushing without systemic features. Management is to stop the transfusion, administer chlorphenamine (an antihistamine), and once the urticaria has resolved the transfusion can be restarted slowly. If anaphylaxis develops (bronchospasm, hypotension, stridor) — the transfusion must be stopped and IM adrenaline 0.5 mg given. Future transfusions may be given with prophylactic antihistamine.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Blood Transfusion — Clinical Practice','hard','mcq','A trauma patient receives 12 units of packed red blood cells within 6 hours following massive haemorrhage from a splenic rupture. His ionised calcium drops to 0.82 mmol/L and he develops tetany. Why has hypocalcaemia developed and how should it be treated?','["A) Massive transfusion causes dilutional coagulopathy washing out calcium from plasma — replace with oral calcium tablets", "B) Citrate used as an anticoagulant in stored blood chelates ionised calcium — treat with IV calcium gluconate 10 mL of 10% solution", "C) Transfused red cells destroy parathyroid hormone — give synthetic PTH replacement", "D) Hyperphosphataemia from lysed red cells binds calcium — give phosphate binders"]','B','Stored blood products contain citrate as an anticoagulant, which chelates (binds) ionised calcium causing hypocalcaemia during massive transfusion when citrate is infused faster than the liver can metabolise it. Hypocalcaemia causes impaired cardiac contractility, prolonged QT interval, and tetany. Treatment is IV calcium gluconate (10 mL of 10% solution, i.e., 2.2 mmol calcium) given slowly IV, repeated as needed with ionised calcium monitoring. In massive transfusion protocols, calcium replacement should be given proactively alongside FFP and platelets in a 1:1:1 ratio.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Blood Transfusion — Clinical Practice','easy','mcq','Which patients should receive CMV-negative blood products to minimise the risk of CMV transmission?','["A) All patients over 65 years old due to declining immunity", "B) All patients receiving their first blood transfusion", "C) Immunocompromised patients (e.g., stem cell transplant recipients, HIV with low CD4 count) and neonates", "D) Patients with autoimmune haemolytic anaemia only"]','C','Cytomegalovirus (CMV) is a herpesvirus carried latently in leucocytes. In immunocompetent individuals, transfused CMV causes only mild or subclinical infection. However, in severely immunocompromised patients — haematopoietic stem cell transplant recipients, organ transplant patients on immunosuppression, HIV patients with low CD4 (<200 cells/µL), and neonates — CMV transmission can cause life-threatening CMV disease (pneumonitis, hepatitis, retinitis). CMV-negative or leucodepleted blood products are used to prevent transmission. Leucodepletion (removing WBCs) also significantly reduces CMV risk.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Blood Transfusion — Clinical Practice','hard','mcq','A 20-year-old man with severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID) awaiting bone marrow transplantation receives a blood transfusion and develops a maculopapular rash, diarrhoea, and liver failure 10 days later. What is this complication and how could it have been prevented?','["A) Acute haemolytic transfusion reaction from ABO mismatch — prevented by double-checking patient identity at bedside", "B) CMV disease from transfusion of CMV-positive blood — prevented with CMV-negative blood", "C) TRALI from donor HLA antibodies — prevented by using male-only plasma for transfusion", "D) Transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease (TA-GvHD) — prevented by using irradiated blood products"]','D','Transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease (TA-GvHD) occurs when viable donor T-lymphocytes in transfused blood engraft in a severely immunocompromised recipient and attack host tissues (skin, gut, liver, bone marrow). It presents 8–30 days post-transfusion with a characteristic rash, diarrhoea, hepatitis, and pancytopenia, and is almost universally fatal (>90% mortality). Prevention requires irradiation of all cellular blood components (gamma irradiation renders donor T-cells unable to proliferate). Indications for irradiated blood include all severe immunodeficiencies, haematological malignancies, stem cell transplant recipients, and transfusions from first-degree relatives.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Blood Transfusion — Clinical Practice','medium','mcq','A patient requires a blood transfusion but has anti-Kell antibodies from a previous transfusion. Why is red cell antibody compatibility important in pre-transfusion testing?','["A) Alloantibodies to non-ABO red cell antigens (e.g., anti-Kell, anti-Rh) can cause delayed or acute haemolytic transfusion reactions when antigen-positive blood is transfused", "B) Kell antibodies only cause haemolytic disease of the newborn — they have no effect on adult transfusion", "C) Antibody screening is only required for patients with sickle cell disease or thalassaemia", "D) Anti-Kell antibodies are clinically insignificant and do not require antigen-negative blood"]','A','Pre-transfusion antibody screening detects clinically significant red cell alloantibodies formed from prior transfusion or pregnancy. Anti-Kell (anti-K) is one of the most clinically significant non-ABO antibodies and can cause acute or delayed haemolytic transfusion reactions when Kell-antigen-positive blood is transfused. Patients with identified antibodies must receive crossmatched blood that is antigen-negative for the relevant antibody. Patients with sickle cell disease or thalassaemia on chronic transfusion programmes are at particular risk of alloimmunisation due to repeated exposure.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Blood Transfusion — Clinical Practice','medium','mcq','A massive transfusion protocol (MTP) is activated for a 40-year-old man with a ruptured aortic aneurysm. The trauma team plans to give 10 units of packed red blood cells. What additional blood products should be co-administered to prevent dilutional coagulopathy?','["A) Platelets only — coagulation factors are not depleted in acute haemorrhage", "B) Fresh frozen plasma (FFP) and platelet concentrate in a 1:1:1 ratio with packed red cells, plus tranexamic acid within 3 hours of injury", "C) Cryoprecipitate alone — it contains all the clotting factors needed", "D) No additional blood products needed if red cells are transfused quickly enough"]','B','Massive transfusion (>10 units pRBC in 24 hours) causes dilutional coagulopathy, acidosis, and hypothermia — the ''''lethal triad.'''' Modern damage control resuscitation uses a 1:1:1 ratio of packed red cells : FFP : platelets to maintain coagulation factor and platelet levels during haemorrhage. Cryoprecipitate is added for fibrinogen replacement when fibrinogen falls below 1.5 g/L. Tranexamic acid (TXA) 1 g IV should be administered within 3 hours of injury (preferably within 1 hour) to inhibit fibrinolysis and reduce mortality, as demonstrated by the CRASH-2 trial.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'));
